WEBVTT

1
00:02:00.064 --> 00:02:01.940
Yes, we received your payment.

2
00:02:02.525 --> 00:02:08.656
No, it wasn't late, but it was for $78.13,
and the bill was for $78.31.

3
00:02:10.241 --> 00:02:12.326
Okay, great.

4
00:02:15.204 --> 00:02:17.248
No, thank you.

5
00:03:04.003 --> 00:03:05.713
I think it's the intake manifold.

6
00:03:08.382 --> 00:03:10.843
- Was I right?
- Not even close.

7
00:03:12.511 --> 00:03:15.973
I think I just like saying "intake manifold."

8
00:03:16.056 --> 00:03:18.684
Maybe when I get a job, I'll get Triple A.

9
00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.644
I thought you worked at the water company.

10
00:03:20.728 --> 00:03:21.687
Temping.

11
00:03:23.397 --> 00:03:26.233
- How's your parents?
- They're doing okay.

12
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.779
I should, um, get going,
actually, for dinner.

13
00:03:30.863 --> 00:03:34.825
But, um, we should make a plan.
I'll send you a text.

14
00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:38.579
Or we could do that now
since we're both here.

15
00:03:38.662 --> 00:03:41.540
- What?
- Nothing.

16
00:03:41.624 --> 00:03:43.083
Okay.

17
00:03:48.005 --> 00:03:49.757
<i>Nice of Mercer.</i>

18
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.800
I always thought you two would make a good...

19
00:03:51.884 --> 00:03:54.136
- Mom.
- Couple.

20
00:03:56.805 --> 00:03:58.807
Boy from the neighborhood?

21
00:03:58.891 --> 00:04:00.142
That would've been cute.

22
00:04:02.686 --> 00:04:04.230
Remember when he fixed the roof?

23
00:04:04.313 --> 00:04:06.273
Stop.

24
00:04:06.357 --> 00:04:08.525
- He's not...
- I know, I know.

25
00:04:17.826 --> 00:04:18.661
Mm.

26
00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.914
Any word on the insurance?

27
00:04:21.997 --> 00:04:27.002
Oh, they won't cover the cost
of IGIV. It's $40,000.

28
00:04:27.086 --> 00:04:29.546
So, you know, surprise, surprise.
It's not on their list.

29
00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.423
Oh, enough of all that.

30
00:04:32.841 --> 00:04:33.842
Come on, honey.

31
00:04:35.511 --> 00:04:37.763
- Help me up.
- Okay.

32
00:04:39.848 --> 00:04:41.475
Here. You got it?

33
00:04:41.558 --> 00:04:42.851
Thanks, Mae.

34
00:04:42.935 --> 00:04:43.894
- Okay?
- Yeah.

35
00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:46.480
That's it.

36
00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.775
Well, not so fast.

37
00:04:55.656 --> 00:04:57.741
Okay and turn... just...

38
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.329
- Ready?
- Yeah. I'm good.

39
00:05:02.413 --> 00:05:03.497
- Yeah.
- Okay.

40
00:05:10.087 --> 00:05:13.632
- <i>What are you doing right now?</i>
- I'm at work.

41
00:05:13.716 --> 00:05:17.136
I come here each day and in return,
I receive money to buy goods and services.

42
00:05:17.219 --> 00:05:19.054
<i>Ah, well, I've got to be quick.</i>
<i>I'm in Germany.</i>

43
00:05:19.138 --> 00:05:20.764
- Why are you in Germany?
- <i>Regulatory mess.</i>

44
00:05:20.848 --> 00:05:24.351
<i>Those guys did not screw around,</i>
<i>but listen, I got you an interview.</i>

45
00:05:24.435 --> 00:05:26.312
- What?
- <i>I got you an interview tomorrow.</i>

46
00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:32.267
- <i>Yes, I did.</i>
- Really? No.

47
00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.487
<i>Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes, I did, tomorrow.</i>

48
00:05:35.571 --> 00:05:37.656
Holy shit, Annie, I love you.

49
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.658
<i>Remember, it's just customer experience.</i>

50
00:05:39.742 --> 00:05:41.660
<i>So answer phones, help people,</i>
<i>what you're already doing</i>

51
00:05:41.744 --> 00:05:42.870
<i>but now a real company.</i>

52
00:05:42.953 --> 00:05:44.330
I know, it's perfect.

53
00:05:44.413 --> 00:05:46.081
<i>Tons of people started there.</i>

54
00:05:46.165 --> 00:05:48.042
I know, I know. Thank you.

55
00:05:48.125 --> 00:05:50.753
<i>Stop saying that.</i>
<i>We're hiring a hundred people this month.</i>

56
00:05:50.836 --> 00:05:53.714
- <i>One of them may as well be you.</i>
- Right, good.

57
00:05:53.797 --> 00:05:56.300
<i>This doesn't mean you're in.</i>
<i>You have to kill the interview.</i>

58
00:05:56.383 --> 00:05:58.385
<i>Know the Circle inside out.</i>
<i>I'm back next week.</i>

59
00:05:58.469 --> 00:06:01.055
<i>By then, you'll be hired</i>
<i>if you kill the interview.</i>

60
00:06:01.138 --> 00:06:02.431
Okay, I will.

61
00:06:02.514 --> 00:06:04.224
<i>You can do it, Mae. Own it!</i>

62
00:06:17.446 --> 00:06:21.450
How would you describe
what the Circle is to say,

63
00:06:21.533 --> 00:06:23.994
- your grandmother?
- Take True You, for example.

64
00:06:25.162 --> 00:06:29.917
It combined everything that was separate
and sloppy into one account.

65
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.630
No more forgetting your password,
multiple identities, identity theft.

66
00:06:34.713 --> 00:06:37.925
You use your real name,
which is tied to your credit card.

67
00:06:38.008 --> 00:06:39.176
I think Grandma's falling asleep.

68
00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:44.181
Okay.

69
00:06:47.518 --> 00:06:50.896
Before True You, it was like
you needed a different vehicle

70
00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:52.773
for every single one of your errands,

71
00:06:52.856 --> 00:06:54.858
and no one should have to own
87 different cars.

72
00:06:54.942 --> 00:06:56.527
It doesn't make sense.

73
00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.656
It's the chaos of the Web
made elegant and simple.

74
00:07:00.739 --> 00:07:03.200
Speed round. Paul or John?

75
00:07:03.283 --> 00:07:04.243
Early Paul, late John.

76
00:07:04.326 --> 00:07:07.746
- Mario or Sonic?
- Early Sonic, late Mario.

77
00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.540
Introspection or communication?

78
00:07:10.999 --> 00:07:12.709
- Uh...
- Mae.

79
00:07:12.793 --> 00:07:15.420
- Sorry, I'm thinking about it.
- So that's introspection.

80
00:07:15.504 --> 00:07:17.422
- Sushi or Soylent?
- Sushi.

81
00:07:17.506 --> 00:07:18.632
Ever owned a Velcro wallet?

82
00:07:20.008 --> 00:07:21.844
- Yes.
- Velcro shoes?

83
00:07:21.927 --> 00:07:24.471
- Yes.
- Velcro... pants?

84
00:07:24.555 --> 00:07:25.806
Yes.

85
00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:28.600
What was the name of the receptionist
who checked you in this morning?

86
00:07:28.684 --> 00:07:29.685
Um...

87
00:07:35.065 --> 00:07:37.359
- Linda.
- Joan Baez or Joan Crawford?

88
00:07:37.443 --> 00:07:38.902
- Joan Didion.
- "Gif" or "jif"?

89
00:07:38.986 --> 00:07:41.989
I'm not answering that.
There's too much at stake.

90
00:07:42.072 --> 00:07:43.740
Would you like to go out with me?

91
00:07:46.827 --> 00:07:48.370
That's very inappropriate.

92
00:07:48.454 --> 00:07:51.457
Good. Quality or convenience?

93
00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.208
Not mutually exclusive.

94
00:07:53.292 --> 00:07:55.377
Needs of the society
or needs of the individual?

95
00:07:55.461 --> 00:07:57.546
- Should be the same.
- You're most scared of?

96
00:07:59.965 --> 00:08:01.675
Unfulfilled potential.

97
00:08:18.984 --> 00:08:22.154
All right, heliport, gym, daycare,

98
00:08:22.237 --> 00:08:24.490
- organic farm, pétanque.
- Pétanque?

99
00:08:24.573 --> 00:08:27.284
- Oh, it's like bocce but French.
- Oh.

100
00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:30.537
Tetherball, lap pool, social pool, doga.

101
00:08:34.249 --> 00:08:38.629
Kennel, nightclub, salon, maker space,

102
00:08:38.712 --> 00:08:42.591
rock climbing, trampoline,
clinic, volleyball.

103
00:08:42.674 --> 00:08:43.967
Was that...? Was that the...?

104
00:08:44.051 --> 00:08:47.137
Content people, news, arts, film, engineers,

105
00:08:47.221 --> 00:08:50.265
management and me, advertising and you.

106
00:08:53.685 --> 00:08:54.728
All right, let's go.

107
00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:58.148
Your legs are so long.

108
00:09:04.321 --> 00:09:05.855
What?

109
00:09:06.439 --> 00:09:09.368
It's all on sensors. The elevator
reads your ID and says "hello."

110
00:09:09.451 --> 00:09:13.330
We do that for visitors mostly.
They're usually impressed.

111
00:09:13.413 --> 00:09:14.915
Annie, that's the worst picture of me ever.

112
00:09:14.998 --> 00:09:17.417
Oh, come on... Oh. Oh, dear.

113
00:09:17.501 --> 00:09:19.002
- Danny!
- What's up?

114
00:09:19.086 --> 00:09:20.379
You remember Dan? I'm handing you off.

115
00:09:20.462 --> 00:09:21.880
- You're all grown-up.
-Oh.

116
00:09:21.964 --> 00:09:25.676
I have the privilege and the honor
to give you your brand new tablet, Mae.

117
00:09:25.759 --> 00:09:27.219
And I sent the password to your phone.

118
00:09:27.302 --> 00:09:29.096
- Oh, my God. It has my name on it.
- Uh-huh.

119
00:09:29.179 --> 00:09:31.348
Oh, this is too sad.
You take care of my little girl.

120
00:09:31.431 --> 00:09:33.267
I will.

121
00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.894
If we don't give customers
a human and humane experience,

122
00:09:35.978 --> 00:09:38.313
we have no customers, it's pretty elemental.

123
00:09:38.397 --> 00:09:39.690
Jared will be doing your training.

124
00:09:39.773 --> 00:09:43.402
I oversee the team,
and Jared oversees the unit.

125
00:09:43.485 --> 00:09:46.196
As you know, you'll be doing
straight-up customer maintenance

126
00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:47.990
for the smaller advertisers.

127
00:09:48.073 --> 00:09:50.075
You work on your portable here.

128
00:09:50.158 --> 00:09:51.868
They send a message to customer experience,

129
00:09:51.952 --> 00:09:54.371
and it gets routed over to one of you.

130
00:09:54.454 --> 00:09:56.957
Random at first, but once
you start working with a customer,

131
00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.168
they'll continue to be routed over to you
for the sake of continuity.

132
00:10:00.252 --> 00:10:02.296
Um, you get the query, figure out the answer,

133
00:10:02.379 --> 00:10:05.591
you write them back,
and that is the core of it.

134
00:10:05.674 --> 00:10:07.634
Simple enough in theory. So far so good?

135
00:10:07.718 --> 00:10:09.469
- Uh, yeah.
- Cool.

136
00:10:09.553 --> 00:10:12.264
So let's say you've answered
a client's question,

137
00:10:12.347 --> 00:10:13.515
and they seem satisfied.

138
00:10:13.599 --> 00:10:16.101
Now, it's not just that you get a smile
or a frown, though that is part of it.

139
00:10:16.184 --> 00:10:18.645
You get your smile...
it's almost always a smile.

140
00:10:18.729 --> 00:10:21.231
Um, that is when you send the survey.

141
00:10:21.315 --> 00:10:24.735
The survey is a set of quick questions
about, you know, your overall service,

142
00:10:24.818 --> 00:10:27.988
how you did,
and they send that immediately back,

143
00:10:28.071 --> 00:10:31.158
and you get to see how you did right here.
That is the rating. Uh, you know what?

144
00:10:31.241 --> 00:10:32.534
Let's just do a live one.

145
00:10:33.702 --> 00:10:35.245
- Excuse me.
- Sorry.

146
00:10:35.329 --> 00:10:36.688
Okay.

147
00:10:38.081 --> 00:10:40.034
Have a nice day.

148
00:10:43.503 --> 00:10:45.005
That's pretty good, 99 is good,

149
00:10:45.088 --> 00:10:49.092
but I can't help wondering
why it wasn't 100. Let's look.

150
00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.928
Okay.

151
00:10:55.807 --> 00:10:59.186
Boom. Sometimes you just encounter someone
who isn't really sensitive to the metrics.

152
00:10:59.269 --> 00:11:03.023
So it's important to ask them
and make sure you get that clarity.

153
00:11:03.106 --> 00:11:06.693
And we're back to a perfect score, yeah.

154
00:11:06.777 --> 00:11:08.737
Oh, wow.

155
00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.031
All right, are you ready
to do this on your own?

156
00:11:11.114 --> 00:11:13.324
- Yes.
- Awesome.

157
00:11:59.413 --> 00:12:00.956
Excuse me. I'm terribly sorry.

158
00:12:01.957 --> 00:12:02.958
Hey, how's it going?

159
00:12:03.041 --> 00:12:04.709
- I thought you were in Amsterdam.
- Oh, just for a day.

160
00:12:04.793 --> 00:12:06.253
Then London, then New York, now here.

161
00:12:06.336 --> 00:12:08.547
- Have you slept?
- A few hours on the plane.

162
00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.924
A couple pills, I'm good. So you? First week.

163
00:12:12.007 --> 00:12:14.219
What? You got an 87 average.

164
00:12:14.302 --> 00:12:16.596
- That's like a solid B.
- How long till I'm fired?

165
00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.640
Listen, when I first started,
it took me a few weeks

166
00:12:18.723 --> 00:12:20.225
to really get the hang of it.
Believe me, okay?

167
00:12:20.308 --> 00:12:22.519
Believe me, you're doing fine.
You're good, all right?

168
00:12:22.602 --> 00:12:23.603
Mm-hm.

169
00:12:23.687 --> 00:12:25.271
Hello, I'm Eamon Bailey.

170
00:12:28.834 --> 00:12:30.267
Hey.

171
00:12:33.155 --> 00:12:36.199
Thank you, thank you.
So glad to see you all here.

172
00:12:36.283 --> 00:12:39.786
Now a bunch of you are new
to the company since I last spoke,

173
00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.997
so can the guppies all stand up?

174
00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:45.250
- Right, that's you.
- Come on, guppies, just stand up.

175
00:12:45.333 --> 00:12:46.585
- Whoo!
- Guppies, stand up, come on.

176
00:12:48.086 --> 00:12:49.629
Congratulations, nice to have you.

177
00:12:49.713 --> 00:12:54.426
Now "guppies" is just an affectionate term
for you baby fishies.

178
00:12:54.509 --> 00:12:57.345
Soon you will all grow to become powerful...

179
00:12:57.429 --> 00:12:59.848
- You're adorable.
- I hate you.

180
00:12:59.931 --> 00:13:02.893
...the fish that guppies grow up
into being...

181
00:13:02.976 --> 00:13:05.103
What do they grow up into?

182
00:13:05.187 --> 00:13:07.147
Anyway, all right, this is Dream Friday,

183
00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.026
the latest in our long tradition
of presenting our newest innovations.

184
00:13:11.109 --> 00:13:14.112
The first Dream Friday featured Ty Lafitte,

185
00:13:14.196 --> 00:13:16.740
the inventor of True You
and our founding father.

186
00:13:16.823 --> 00:13:20.243
And usually, we feature one
of our engineers or our visionaries

187
00:13:20.327 --> 00:13:22.913
but today unfortunately,
it's just me and for that,

188
00:13:22.996 --> 00:13:24.581
I apologize in advance.

189
00:13:24.664 --> 00:13:26.374
We love you, Eamon!

190
00:13:26.458 --> 00:13:27.626
Thanks, Mom.

191
00:13:30.086 --> 00:13:31.588
Now you might not have seen me around campus

192
00:13:31.671 --> 00:13:33.173
for the last few days,
and maybe you were wondering

193
00:13:33.256 --> 00:13:34.716
just where the heck I've been.

194
00:13:34.799 --> 00:13:35.926
Surfing!

195
00:13:37.844 --> 00:13:41.223
You got me! Yes, I've been surfing.

196
00:13:41.306 --> 00:13:43.642
And that's part
of what I'm here to talk about.

197
00:13:43.725 --> 00:13:46.061
I love to surf and in the past,

198
00:13:46.144 --> 00:13:50.815
I would have to wake up
and call the local surf shop

199
00:13:50.899 --> 00:13:54.069
to ask about the local breaks,
and pretty soon,

200
00:13:54.152 --> 00:13:57.564
the local surf shop
stopped answering their phone.

201
00:13:58.747 --> 00:14:01.418
And then I got a cell phone,

202
00:14:01.501 --> 00:14:04.371
and I could call up my buddies
who were already out

203
00:14:04.454 --> 00:14:06.623
at the beach before me, and I could ask them,

204
00:14:06.706 --> 00:14:10.043
but they, too,
stopped answering their phones.

205
00:14:11.127 --> 00:14:12.546
And then came...

206
00:14:13.588 --> 00:14:14.589
the Internet.

207
00:14:17.676 --> 00:14:21.596
Some geniuses set up cameras on the beaches,

208
00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:25.267
so I could log on
and get some very crude images

209
00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.352
of the waves at Stinson Beach.

210
00:14:27.435 --> 00:14:31.147
It was almost worse
than calling the surf shop.

211
00:14:31.231 --> 00:14:33.024
Here's how it used to look.

212
00:14:34.609 --> 00:14:36.778
Almost useless, right?

213
00:14:36.861 --> 00:14:39.072
But now let's refresh that page

214
00:14:39.155 --> 00:14:43.201
and show the site
with our new Circle video delivery.

215
00:14:45.069 --> 00:14:47.247
Now that looks pretty good, doesn't it?

216
00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.332
Yeah, maybe I should be out there right now.

217
00:14:50.016 --> 00:14:51.751
But would it surprise you to know

218
00:14:51.835 --> 00:14:56.131
that this is not coming from some big camera

219
00:14:56.214 --> 00:14:59.301
but actually just one of these?

220
00:15:00.677 --> 00:15:04.306
This camera needs no wires.

221
00:15:04.389 --> 00:15:08.101
It transmits this image via satellite.

222
00:15:08.184 --> 00:15:10.353
I set up that camera this morning.

223
00:15:10.437 --> 00:15:14.232
I stuck it to a tree near the dunes
with no permit, nothing,

224
00:15:14.316 --> 00:15:16.484
in fact, no one even knows it's there.

225
00:15:16.568 --> 00:15:18.486
And actually, I was pretty busy this morning

226
00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:23.158
because here's Rodeo Beach, Montera,

227
00:15:23.241 --> 00:15:25.452
Ocean Beach,

228
00:15:25.535 --> 00:15:27.037
Fort Point.

229
00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:28.204
This is my front yard.

230
00:15:29.539 --> 00:15:31.791
And here's my backyard.

231
00:15:31.875 --> 00:15:33.043
Here's traffic on the 101.

232
00:15:33.126 --> 00:15:35.253
Oh, did you think this was just a camera?

233
00:15:35.337 --> 00:15:38.506
No, welcome to real time analytic processing.

234
00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:42.260
When you see this, you're also getting this.

235
00:15:45.138 --> 00:15:48.391
Air quality, traffic volume,
weather patterns, biometrics,

236
00:15:48.475 --> 00:15:53.480
facial recognition, saved and searchable,
all to serve you better.

237
00:15:53.563 --> 00:15:58.693
My friends, we are looking
at retailing these in just a few months

238
00:15:58.777 --> 00:16:01.196
for less than a pair of jeans.

239
00:16:01.879 --> 00:16:04.324
And I'm not talking about fancy jeans.

240
00:16:04.407 --> 00:16:07.202
I'm talking about the ones like I'm wearing.

241
00:16:07.285 --> 00:16:08.328
Wow!

242
00:16:08.411 --> 00:16:13.208
Now, Circlers... do you like to share?

243
00:16:13.291 --> 00:16:15.001
Sharing is caring!

244
00:16:15.085 --> 00:16:17.921
Sharing is caring.
I thought you might say that.

245
00:16:18.004 --> 00:16:19.422
- Sharing is caring?
- Sharing is caring.

246
00:16:19.506 --> 00:16:24.719
I have a dozen helpers all over the world
right now carrying these cameras.

247
00:16:24.803 --> 00:16:27.597
Imagine the human rights implications.

248
00:16:27.681 --> 00:16:31.977
Activists no longer have to hold up a camera.

249
00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:34.980
Now it's as easy as
gluing a camera to a wall,

250
00:16:35.063 --> 00:16:37.649
and actually, we've done just that.

251
00:16:38.984 --> 00:16:40.485
Do you see any cameras here?

252
00:16:41.528 --> 00:16:42.654
No.

253
00:16:42.737 --> 00:16:48.201
We have 144 cameras in place here,
and it took us just a day to do it,

254
00:16:48.284 --> 00:16:49.619
and they're virtually invisible.

255
00:16:49.703 --> 00:16:53.623
They come in every color,
so camouflaging is a cinch.

256
00:16:53.707 --> 00:16:55.875
This is my favorite. Whoa!

257
00:16:57.159 --> 00:16:59.546
Nice catch. Sign her up.

258
00:17:00.129 --> 00:17:04.426
So now, the entire world is watching.

259
00:17:04.509 --> 00:17:06.094
You know, I agree with the Hague,

260
00:17:06.177 --> 00:17:08.888
with the human rights activists
all over the world.

261
00:17:08.972 --> 00:17:10.932
There needs to be accountability.

262
00:17:11.016 --> 00:17:14.811
Tyrants and terrorists can no longer hide.

263
00:17:14.894 --> 00:17:19.858
We will see them. We will hear them.
We will hear and see everything.

264
00:17:19.941 --> 00:17:22.360
If it happens, we'll know.

265
00:17:23.987 --> 00:17:27.657
We're calling it... SeeChange.

266
00:17:29.784 --> 00:17:32.120
SeeChange.

267
00:17:34.456 --> 00:17:36.875
Oh, we'll know the good things, too.

268
00:17:36.958 --> 00:17:39.753
- Aw.
- Aw.

269
00:17:39.836 --> 00:17:42.088
We will see it all.

270
00:17:42.172 --> 00:17:45.300
Because knowing is good.

271
00:17:45.383 --> 00:17:47.886
But knowing everything is better.

272
00:17:55.185 --> 00:17:57.729
Whoo! Yeah!

273
00:18:06.613 --> 00:18:09.449
Good thing you guys like
to keep things modest.

274
00:18:09.532 --> 00:18:10.867
What's this party even for?

275
00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.661
SeeChange. Summer solstice.
Everything. Nothing.

276
00:18:13.745 --> 00:18:16.206
Come on, let's be the only ones
who know how to dance!

277
00:18:16.289 --> 00:18:18.350
My God, is that Beck?

278
00:18:22.128 --> 00:18:26.216
<i># Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh #</i>

279
00:18:26.299 --> 00:18:29.552
<i># Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh #</i>

280
00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:34.599
<i># Come on out of your dreams #</i>

281
00:18:34.682 --> 00:18:37.310
<i># And wake up from your rêverie #</i>

282
00:18:39.145 --> 00:18:42.857
<i># Time is here, don't go to sleep #</i>

283
00:18:42.941 --> 00:18:45.610
<i># Streets are running on the brink #</i>

284
00:18:46.861 --> 00:18:49.364
So Bailey, what's he actually like?

285
00:18:49.447 --> 00:18:50.865
You're in the gang of 40.

286
00:18:50.949 --> 00:18:53.201
Yeah, but I don't get a ton
of alone time with him.

287
00:18:53.284 --> 00:18:55.537
I mean, you know,
a lot of people work in the White House.

288
00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.872
How many people really get
to know the President?

289
00:18:57.956 --> 00:19:01.000
Oh, actually, do you wanna see something?

290
00:19:01.084 --> 00:19:03.128
Oh, my God. You need to see this. Follow me.

291
00:19:03.211 --> 00:19:06.172
<i># Here we are #</i>

292
00:19:08.299 --> 00:19:10.927
Here. This is it.

293
00:19:15.765 --> 00:19:17.809
Nice knocker.

294
00:19:19.642 --> 00:19:21.104
Holy shit, right?

295
00:19:22.105 --> 00:19:23.347
Right?

296
00:19:24.232 --> 00:19:27.068
I think all of these have gotta
be first editions?

297
00:19:27.152 --> 00:19:30.071
Oh, but, um, you have to give me a verbal
nondisclosure agreement, okay?

298
00:19:30.155 --> 00:19:31.614
- Annie.
- Seriously.

299
00:19:31.698 --> 00:19:35.451
You shouldn't be here and probably
won't ever be here again, so verbal NDA.

300
00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:40.874
- I promise not to disclose anything.
- Good.

301
00:19:40.957 --> 00:19:44.002
This is where Bailey and Stenton
make all of their plans.

302
00:19:44.085 --> 00:19:48.631
It's very serious.
I mean, this is high nerd, right?

303
00:19:48.715 --> 00:19:50.216
Oh, no, you probably shouldn't play with...

304
00:19:52.677 --> 00:19:56.681
- Like this?
- You're such a dork.

305
00:19:56.764 --> 00:19:57.765
Well...

306
00:19:58.892 --> 00:20:02.437
- So this is where it all happens.
- To be a fly on the wall, right?

307
00:20:03.855 --> 00:20:05.907
We should get back to the party.

308
00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:08.409
Well, you should at least.

309
00:20:09.903 --> 00:20:12.322
- You're not coming back?
- I've got work.

310
00:20:12.405 --> 00:20:14.908
- Annie, it's like, 11 o'clock.
- I've got Australia in a few minutes.

311
00:20:14.991 --> 00:20:17.827
Ah. A-ha. Actually, a few minutes ago,
but you have fun, okay?

312
00:20:17.911 --> 00:20:20.205
- Annie...
- You're gonna have so much fun.

313
00:20:24.542 --> 00:20:26.502
- You guys are gonna love this.
- Okay.

314
00:20:29.964 --> 00:20:30.798
Whoops.

315
00:20:42.268 --> 00:20:45.939
So I heard there's a party around here.

316
00:20:46.022 --> 00:20:48.358
You know, if that's, like...

317
00:20:48.441 --> 00:20:50.151
your kind of thing.

318
00:20:50.235 --> 00:20:51.694
Yeah, maybe I'll check it out.

319
00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.282
It's probably not as interesting
as whatever's on your phone, though.

320
00:20:59.535 --> 00:21:00.745
Okay, probably not.

321
00:21:06.834 --> 00:21:10.171
- Do you want a drink?
- I do, but they ran out like an hour ago.

322
00:21:10.255 --> 00:21:12.674
I think they did too, but shh.

323
00:21:16.261 --> 00:21:18.846
Are you serious? Whoa.

324
00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:22.225
- You have glasses in there, too?
- It's my party trick.

325
00:21:24.727 --> 00:21:27.063
- Oh.
- All right.

326
00:21:28.273 --> 00:21:29.107
Thank you.

327
00:21:33.903 --> 00:21:34.737
You're new here.

328
00:21:36.281 --> 00:21:39.534
- You are, right?
- How can you tell that?

329
00:21:39.617 --> 00:21:41.995
- I don't know, I just guessed.
- That's really embarrassing.

330
00:21:43.454 --> 00:21:45.290
I'm sorry.

331
00:21:45.373 --> 00:21:47.250
What do you think?

332
00:21:47.333 --> 00:21:49.252
- Of this?
- Yeah.

333
00:21:52.213 --> 00:21:54.215
I'm excited.

334
00:21:58.852 --> 00:22:00.305
- I'm sorry.
- Okay.

335
00:22:00.388 --> 00:22:01.848
It's just everyone that works at the Circle

336
00:22:01.931 --> 00:22:04.892
is so excited to be here,

337
00:22:04.976 --> 00:22:08.855
and they just want to tell everyone
about it all the time. It's, you know...

338
00:22:08.938 --> 00:22:10.440
It's like, "Here, try this Kool-Aid!"

339
00:22:10.523 --> 00:22:12.317
- Oh, Kool-Aid?
- "It's delicious. You'll love it."

340
00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.068
- Oh, wow!
- I love Kool-Aid.

341
00:22:14.152 --> 00:22:15.945
Don't you love Kool-Aid?

342
00:22:17.155 --> 00:22:20.366
After my last job
where I just tried to, like,

343
00:22:20.450 --> 00:22:22.660
calm down angry people all day...

344
00:22:23.703 --> 00:22:25.705
I'm just grateful to be here.

345
00:22:25.788 --> 00:22:28.624
I got to go put out a fire.

346
00:22:28.708 --> 00:22:32.253
- Sounds exciting.
- Very. So, uh...

347
00:22:32.337 --> 00:22:33.338
I'll leave you to it.

348
00:22:35.214 --> 00:22:38.259
Have a drink, have a good time,
and stay excited.

349
00:22:38.343 --> 00:22:39.469
You're at the Circle.

350
00:22:46.017 --> 00:22:48.895
Did he tell me his name? I forgot it.

351
00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:54.108
- Have a good night, guppy.
- Oh! Thanks.

352
00:22:54.192 --> 00:22:57.111
<i>Last shuttle will be leaving in five minutes.</i>

353
00:23:24.305 --> 00:23:28.101
I'm telling you Mae-Mae...
there's something about you.

354
00:23:28.184 --> 00:23:29.977
You have a glow.

355
00:23:30.061 --> 00:23:33.106
- You do.
- I do not have a glow.

356
00:23:33.189 --> 00:23:35.192
Like you're with child.

357
00:23:36.275 --> 00:23:38.027
Ignore him.

358
00:23:38.111 --> 00:23:39.987
So it's been great?

359
00:23:40.071 --> 00:23:42.115
I don't think I ever wanna work
anywhere else.

360
00:23:43.116 --> 00:23:44.992
I told, um, Carolina's mom.

361
00:23:45.076 --> 00:23:47.286
I might've let your salary slip.

362
00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:48.955
- Oh, Mom.
- What?

363
00:23:49.038 --> 00:23:53.042
It's been fun to casually
insert it into conversation.

364
00:23:53.126 --> 00:23:57.130
"My daughter works for the best company
on the planet!

365
00:23:57.213 --> 00:23:59.215
And has full dental."

366
00:23:59.298 --> 00:24:01.592
Please don't. I just got lucky.

367
00:24:01.676 --> 00:24:04.137
- If it wasn't for Annie...
- Annie, how is Annie?

368
00:24:05.263 --> 00:24:06.264
She's good.

369
00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:10.017
- Working too hard.
- What does she do exactly?

370
00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:13.354
She's in the gang of 40.

371
00:24:13.437 --> 00:24:15.439
Uh-huh.

372
00:24:15.523 --> 00:24:17.066
She's in on all the big decisions,

373
00:24:18.568 --> 00:24:20.862
deals with regulatory issues
in other countries.

374
00:24:21.988 --> 00:24:23.823
That sounds important.

375
00:24:25.449 --> 00:24:29.996
Mae, hon, why don't you get your daddy
another beer?

376
00:24:30.079 --> 00:24:31.080
Sure, Dad.

377
00:24:47.013 --> 00:24:48.764
Your hair.

378
00:24:48.848 --> 00:24:50.808
It was a phase.

379
00:24:50.892 --> 00:24:52.602
It was a cute phase.

380
00:24:53.853 --> 00:24:55.605
You good?

381
00:24:55.688 --> 00:24:57.481
I'm good.

382
00:24:57.565 --> 00:24:59.525
You should come visit me at work sometime.

383
00:24:59.609 --> 00:25:00.484
At the Circle?

384
00:25:01.527 --> 00:25:03.237
I'll give that idea a smile.

385
00:25:03.321 --> 00:25:04.780
Hey...

386
00:25:04.864 --> 00:25:07.491
They might think I was there
to clean the toilets.

387
00:25:07.575 --> 00:25:10.328
Come on. They'd love you.

388
00:25:10.411 --> 00:25:12.914
All those tech guys secretly wish
they knew how to use a hand saw,

389
00:25:12.997 --> 00:25:14.874
make something out of wood.

390
00:25:14.957 --> 00:25:17.043
That really sums me up pretty well.

391
00:25:17.126 --> 00:25:20.463
- It could be worse, I guess.
- You know what I mean.

392
00:25:20.546 --> 00:25:23.007
They want you to tell them
about your secret hiking spots.

393
00:25:23.090 --> 00:25:26.594
So they could put them on True You
and ruin it forever?

394
00:25:26.677 --> 00:25:29.430
Ooh, biting commentary.

395
00:25:29.513 --> 00:25:31.474
I mean, come on, you have to admit, like,

396
00:25:31.557 --> 00:25:34.977
smiles and frowns
used to be for middle school.

397
00:25:35.061 --> 00:25:37.313
Like, "Hey, you know,
do you like stickers and unicorns?"

398
00:25:37.396 --> 00:25:40.233
"Yes, I love stickers and unicorns."
Smile, you know.

399
00:25:40.316 --> 00:25:42.401
And now... now it's everyone.

400
00:25:42.485 --> 00:25:46.364
I can't help it if you're not... social.

401
00:25:46.447 --> 00:25:50.451
I am social enough.
I mean, isn't... this socializing?

402
00:25:51.994 --> 00:25:56.249
I assume you've heard about that senator
that's trying to, like, break you guys up

403
00:25:56.332 --> 00:25:58.960
- citing antitrust laws, right?
- Give me a break, Mercer.

404
00:25:59.043 --> 00:26:01.420
Don't you have to go
and chop some wood or something?

405
00:26:06.467 --> 00:26:08.052
- Thanks, hon.
- Sure, Dad.

406
00:26:10.137 --> 00:26:11.847
His business is doing well.

407
00:26:11.931 --> 00:26:15.351
Mercer? The antler chandeliers?

408
00:26:15.434 --> 00:26:17.812
They are beautiful, Mae.
He's making one for us.

409
00:26:17.895 --> 00:26:22.024
Did I tell you I'm up to an 87 so far?
They say that's pretty good for a guppy.

410
00:26:23.442 --> 00:26:25.194
Who's a guppy?

411
00:26:25.278 --> 00:26:26.988
Well, that's great, Mae.

412
00:26:29.699 --> 00:26:31.951
- Honey?
- Hm?

413
00:26:32.034 --> 00:26:34.870
Oh. Come on, we'll go in the house, come on.

414
00:26:34.954 --> 00:26:36.831
Dad? Don't worry.

415
00:26:36.914 --> 00:26:38.708
- What happened?
- Please, Mae, let your mother.

416
00:26:38.791 --> 00:26:39.792
Honey, just, just...

417
00:26:39.875 --> 00:26:41.502
Just tell everybody
that Dad's not feeling well,

418
00:26:41.585 --> 00:26:44.463
and they'll understand, really.
It's okay. It's okay. It's okay.

419
00:26:45.147 --> 00:26:46.674
It's okay.

420
00:26:46.757 --> 00:26:48.259
Dad.

421
00:26:48.342 --> 00:26:50.303
Please, Mae.

422
00:26:51.886 --> 00:26:53.640
We got it.

423
00:26:53.723 --> 00:26:54.890
It's okay.

424
00:26:56.642 --> 00:26:57.852
Come on.

425
00:27:44.899 --> 00:27:45.733
Thanks.

426
00:27:51.781 --> 00:27:52.948
See you soon, Mae.

427
00:27:54.241 --> 00:27:55.242
Bye.

428
00:28:17.098 --> 00:28:21.018
<i>Today, I call upon the Senate
Antitrust Subcommittee</i>

429
00:28:21.102 --> 00:28:22.895
<i>to investigate the Circle.</i>

430
00:28:22.978 --> 00:28:27.233
<i>We cannot rely on them to say,</i>
<i>"We're not violating antitrust laws."</i>

431
00:28:27.316 --> 00:28:30.486
<i>I'm sure the Circle</i>
<i>will be glad to cooperate.</i>

432
00:28:30.569 --> 00:28:34.407
<i>We have every confidence</i>
<i>that Senator Williamson's allegations</i>

433
00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:35.491
<i>will be discredited.</i>

434
00:28:35.574 --> 00:28:37.952
You are probably wondering
about your new screen.

435
00:28:38.535 --> 00:28:39.787
Hi, I'm Gina.

436
00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:41.163
- I'm Matt.
- Hi.

437
00:28:41.247 --> 00:28:44.208
Dan said this would be a good moment
to set up all your socials.

438
00:28:44.291 --> 00:28:45.709
- Do you have time?
- Sure.

439
00:28:45.793 --> 00:28:46.794
Great.

440
00:28:46.877 --> 00:28:51.215
I take it last week you were too busy
to set up your company social account,

441
00:28:51.298 --> 00:28:53.634
and I don't think
you've imported your old profile.

442
00:28:53.717 --> 00:28:55.928
Sorry, I've just been
kind of overwhelmed so far.

443
00:28:56.011 --> 00:28:57.805
- Oh.
- No, in a good way.

444
00:28:57.888 --> 00:29:01.725
I just haven't had time yet
for extracurricular stuff.

445
00:29:01.809 --> 00:29:03.227
Wow.

446
00:29:03.310 --> 00:29:05.396
That's so interesting you put it that way.

447
00:29:05.479 --> 00:29:08.441
We actually see your profile
and the activity on it

448
00:29:08.524 --> 00:29:12.611
as integral to your participation here.
This is how your coworkers,

449
00:29:12.695 --> 00:29:16.157
even the ones on the other side of campus,
know who you are.

450
00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.577
Communication certainly
isn't extracurricular, right?

451
00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:21.495
Right. Of course.

452
00:29:21.579 --> 00:29:25.958
We consider you a full knowable person
of unlimited potential

453
00:29:26.041 --> 00:29:28.169
and a crucial member of the community.

454
00:29:28.252 --> 00:29:30.880
But you're such an enigma.

455
00:29:30.963 --> 00:29:31.964
I am?

456
00:29:32.047 --> 00:29:35.718
It's been years since someone
so shrouded in mystery started here.

457
00:29:35.801 --> 00:29:38.429
- Everyone really likes you.
- Your work's been exemplary.

458
00:29:38.512 --> 00:29:40.639
Strong ratings for your first week.

459
00:29:40.723 --> 00:29:43.058
Are you satisfied with your performance?

460
00:29:43.142 --> 00:29:45.644
Uh, I think I can do better.

461
00:29:45.728 --> 00:29:48.397
Good, but it's not all about work, okay?

462
00:29:48.481 --> 00:29:51.442
- It's all also about community.
- Of course.

463
00:29:51.525 --> 00:29:53.944
It's all connected.

464
00:29:54.945 --> 00:29:58.699
But you've had a blip or two when it comes
to meshing with the community,

465
00:29:58.783 --> 00:30:02.161
like your absence at several weekend
or evening events.

466
00:30:02.244 --> 00:30:04.580
Which are, of course, optional.

467
00:30:04.663 --> 00:30:06.624
- I'm sorry?
- Let's start with this weekend.

468
00:30:06.707 --> 00:30:13.005
You left campus at 11:42 p.m. on Friday
and got back 8:46 a.m., Monday.

469
00:30:13.088 --> 00:30:14.590
Was there work on the weekend?

470
00:30:14.673 --> 00:30:16.217
- Oh, no, no!
- I didn't... I'm sorry.

471
00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:17.134
Oh, so sweet.

472
00:30:17.218 --> 00:30:18.886
There wasn't mandatory work on the weekend.

473
00:30:18.969 --> 00:30:22.556
This isn't like a clock in,
clock out type place, thank God!

474
00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:24.391
But, you know,
there were thousands of people here

475
00:30:24.475 --> 00:30:26.310
participating in a hundred
different activities.

476
00:30:26.393 --> 00:30:27.228
So many.

477
00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:31.565
I'm sorry, I, um...

478
00:30:31.649 --> 00:30:35.611
My dad had an... an episode,
and I was home helping out with that.

479
00:30:35.694 --> 00:30:38.364
I'm so sorry to hear that.
Is that related to his MS?

480
00:30:40.074 --> 00:30:42.785
Uh... yeah.

481
00:30:42.868 --> 00:30:44.620
We didn't know anything about this incident.

482
00:30:44.703 --> 00:30:46.997
Did you reach out to any Circlers
during this crisis?

483
00:30:47.081 --> 00:30:51.293
- Annie? Anybody?
- Uh, no. I... No.

484
00:30:51.377 --> 00:30:55.422
Mae, there are four groups here
for staffers dealing with MS,

485
00:30:55.506 --> 00:30:57.591
two of them for children of MS patients.

486
00:30:57.675 --> 00:30:59.927
Have you sought out a group?

487
00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:02.555
- I didn't know.
- Okay.

488
00:31:02.638 --> 00:31:06.725
Can you... talk about what you did
the rest of the weekend?

489
00:31:06.809 --> 00:31:09.395
Nothing, really. I just... I kayaked.

490
00:31:09.478 --> 00:31:12.648
You kayaked? With who?

491
00:31:12.731 --> 00:31:15.317
Nobody. Just on my own.

492
00:31:15.401 --> 00:31:16.902
I kayak.

493
00:31:16.986 --> 00:31:18.153
We could have kayaked together.

494
00:31:18.237 --> 00:31:23.284
Nothing on your profile about kayaking,
no smiles, no ratings, no posts.

495
00:31:23.367 --> 00:31:27.788
I'm no psychologist, but this behavior
sometimes stems from low self-worth.

496
00:31:27.872 --> 00:31:32.376
This is what participation is all about,
bringing you, lifting you up.

497
00:31:32.459 --> 00:31:35.713
Thus, your participation window.

498
00:31:35.796 --> 00:31:39.300
I can't believe you've been here this long
without being on the main social feed.

499
00:31:39.383 --> 00:31:41.510
You're about to have your world rocked.

500
00:31:42.595 --> 00:31:44.555
See, these are all of last week's messages.

501
00:31:44.638 --> 00:31:46.265
That's why there's so many.

502
00:31:46.348 --> 00:31:49.351
- Eight thousand?
- Mm-hm.

503
00:31:49.435 --> 00:31:52.855
Looks like you have a lot
of catching up to do here. A feast!

504
00:31:53.488 --> 00:31:55.031
Have fun.

505
00:31:55.983 --> 00:31:57.818
Oh, forgot one more thing.

506
00:31:57.902 --> 00:32:01.739
This is your participation rank.
Party rank for short.

507
00:32:01.822 --> 00:32:05.868
Some people here call it the
popularity rank, but it's not really that.

508
00:32:05.951 --> 00:32:07.870
It's just an algorithm-generated number

509
00:32:07.953 --> 00:32:10.873
that takes into account
all your activity in the inner circle.

510
00:32:10.956 --> 00:32:11.790
That makes sense?

511
00:32:12.875 --> 00:32:16.420
- I think so.
- Okay. And again, it's just for fun.

512
00:32:16.503 --> 00:32:18.464
- It's just for fun!
- Okay, bye.

513
00:32:21.759 --> 00:32:24.178
Um, I think it's for Tuesday next week?

514
00:32:24.261 --> 00:32:25.930
Is that in Building 74 or...?

515
00:32:26.013 --> 00:32:28.182
- Uh, no. It's actually off campus.
- Okay.

516
00:33:08.597 --> 00:33:09.556
<i>It's been a month.</i>

517
00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:12.393
<i>When are you coming home? We miss you.</i>

518
00:33:12.476 --> 00:33:14.186
I can't. You know.

519
00:33:14.269 --> 00:33:17.147
<i>How's the dorm?</i>
<i>Do you have to clean your own room?</i>

520
00:33:17.231 --> 00:33:19.525
- We have people that do that.
- <i>Ooh, thank God.</i>

521
00:33:19.608 --> 00:33:21.694
How's Dad?

522
00:33:21.777 --> 00:33:24.738
<i>He's okay. He... he fell yesterday.</i>

523
00:33:24.822 --> 00:33:28.325
<i>And I couldn't lift him up,</i>
<i>but thank God Mercer came by.</i>

524
00:33:28.409 --> 00:33:29.618
<i>He's stronger than I thought.</i>

525
00:33:31.328 --> 00:33:32.329
I'm sorry.

526
00:33:33.414 --> 00:33:34.540
I should have been there.

527
00:33:43.007 --> 00:33:44.091
Is that one of his?

528
00:33:45.092 --> 00:33:48.929
- <i>Yeah. I think it's his best work.</i>
- It's awesome.

529
00:33:51.265 --> 00:33:54.560
I gotta go, Mom. I love you.

530
00:33:54.643 --> 00:33:56.729
- <i>Okay, well, bye. Bye.</i>
- Bye.

531
00:34:12.703 --> 00:34:16.331
I'm so sorry about last week.
I've been up my own ass.

532
00:34:16.415 --> 00:34:18.208
And I didn't mean to be a shitty friend.

533
00:34:18.292 --> 00:34:20.753
It's okay. Where are we going?

534
00:34:20.836 --> 00:34:23.297
Listen, I've been looking
into your parents' situation,

535
00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:26.341
and I think... I have an idea.

536
00:34:26.425 --> 00:34:28.260
All right? Yeah?

537
00:34:28.343 --> 00:34:29.511
- Hi.
- Hi.

538
00:34:29.595 --> 00:34:33.390
- This is Jessica. This is Mae.
- Hello, Mae, how are you?

539
00:34:33.474 --> 00:34:36.018
I understand you haven't done
your medical intake yet.

540
00:34:36.101 --> 00:34:38.353
- No.
- Can you drink this for me?

541
00:34:43.525 --> 00:34:44.818
All of it, if you can.

542
00:34:49.656 --> 00:34:53.452
- I told you about Mae's dad's...
- Mm-hm, multiple sclerosis.

543
00:34:53.535 --> 00:34:56.580
I did my post doc on MS.
Love to get some real time data.

544
00:34:57.873 --> 00:34:58.916
Here you go.

545
00:34:59.958 --> 00:35:00.793
Okay.

546
00:35:02.628 --> 00:35:04.922
- How does this feel?
- Warm.

547
00:35:06.882 --> 00:35:09.301
Is this for free?

548
00:35:09.384 --> 00:35:11.220
Of course. You get to keep it.

549
00:35:11.303 --> 00:35:13.138
So, it's going to feel warm for a few days,

550
00:35:13.222 --> 00:35:15.516
then you and the media band
will get used to each other,

551
00:35:15.599 --> 00:35:18.602
but it has to touch the skin to work.

552
00:35:18.685 --> 00:35:21.355
Waterproof. No need to take it off.

553
00:35:21.438 --> 00:35:23.065
Let's activate it, shall we?

554
00:35:24.942 --> 00:35:25.776
Is that the...?

555
00:35:25.859 --> 00:35:29.279
Well, it's syncing up with the sensor
you ingested a second ago.

556
00:35:32.699 --> 00:35:34.451
And now...

557
00:35:34.535 --> 00:35:36.370
Let's have you walk around the room.

558
00:35:41.041 --> 00:35:41.959
Briskly.

559
00:35:43.836 --> 00:35:47.172
It'll collect data on your heart rate,
blood pressure, cholesterol,

560
00:35:47.256 --> 00:35:52.970
sleep duration, sleep quality,
digestive efficiency, on and on.

561
00:35:53.053 --> 00:35:55.848
And all that data is stored
in the Cloud and in your tablet,

562
00:35:55.931 --> 00:35:59.434
anywhere you want, and of course, it's all
collated with the rest of the Circle.

563
00:36:00.602 --> 00:36:02.062
You'd be amazed at the patterns we see

564
00:36:02.146 --> 00:36:04.773
and the problems we can avoid
when we have all that data.

565
00:36:04.857 --> 00:36:07.192
Speaking of which, I was thinking
it would make sense for...

566
00:36:07.276 --> 00:36:09.987
Mae's folks to come on
the Circle health plan.

567
00:36:10.070 --> 00:36:11.697
Uh, wait, what?

568
00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:14.199
Wouldn't be the first time,
and I'd really like to get

569
00:36:14.283 --> 00:36:15.492
real time data from your dad.

570
00:36:15.576 --> 00:36:18.704
Sit back down. Can you get in touch with HR,

571
00:36:18.787 --> 00:36:19.872
- clear it with them?
- Yep.

572
00:36:19.955 --> 00:36:21.665
- Tell them I say it's a go.
- Absolutely.

573
00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:25.919
We'll send someone out to set them up, okay?

574
00:36:28.046 --> 00:36:30.132
<i>Honey, this is gonna save our lives.</i>

575
00:36:30.215 --> 00:36:33.802
- <i>Thank you. And thank Annie too.</i>
- They thank you.

576
00:36:33.886 --> 00:36:35.387
Aw.

577
00:36:35.470 --> 00:36:37.890
<i>This is amazing. Call you later, okay?</i>

578
00:36:37.973 --> 00:36:40.809
Okay. I gotta go. Big event here on campus.

579
00:36:40.893 --> 00:36:42.060
- <i>Exciting.</i>
- I know.

580
00:36:42.144 --> 00:36:43.937
- <i>I love you.</i>
- I love you, too.

581
00:36:44.021 --> 00:36:46.398
- Bye, Mom!
- Bye.

582
00:36:48.358 --> 00:36:51.445
- Thank you.
- Oh. You're welcome.

583
00:36:53.363 --> 00:36:56.032
All right. You okay?

584
00:36:56.816 --> 00:36:58.826
Okay. Come on.

585
00:37:05.042 --> 00:37:09.421
Openness is something
we advocate here at the Circle.

586
00:37:10.464 --> 00:37:11.465
We live it.

587
00:37:11.548 --> 00:37:15.302
We demonstrate our commitment
to openness every day.

588
00:37:16.887 --> 00:37:20.057
Our government, not so much.

589
00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:23.936
We expect openness, and we get obfuscation.

590
00:37:24.019 --> 00:37:25.812
We get doublespeak.

591
00:37:25.896 --> 00:37:31.360
No wonder Congress' approval rating
is currently at 11 percent.

592
00:37:33.028 --> 00:37:36.156
Now, I'm sure a lot of you
have heard about a certain senator

593
00:37:36.240 --> 00:37:42.329
who it was revealed has been involved
in some... very unsavory business.

594
00:37:43.372 --> 00:37:47.751
What are we to learn from this?
We need accountability.

595
00:37:47.834 --> 00:37:49.920
We need openness.

596
00:37:50.003 --> 00:37:53.590
We need to know
what the people who represent us

597
00:37:53.674 --> 00:37:57.302
are doing with their time on our dime.

598
00:37:59.513 --> 00:38:01.223
To serve us better.

599
00:38:01.306 --> 00:38:02.891
Well, I'm happy to report

600
00:38:02.975 --> 00:38:08.188
that there's a woman
who is taking all of this very seriously

601
00:38:08.272 --> 00:38:13.277
and is taking some pretty dramatic steps
to address the problem.

602
00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:16.280
So please join me in welcoming

603
00:38:16.363 --> 00:38:20.033
Olivia Santos, Congresswoman for District 14.

604
00:38:24.496 --> 00:38:25.497
That's right, Tom.

605
00:38:26.581 --> 00:38:30.585
I am as concerned as you are
about the need for citizens to know

606
00:38:30.669 --> 00:38:33.672
what their elected leaders are doing.

607
00:38:33.755 --> 00:38:39.803
So I intend to show exactly
how democracy can and should be.

608
00:38:44.766 --> 00:38:48.145
Starting today, my every meeting,

609
00:38:48.228 --> 00:38:52.941
my every phone call
and e-mail will be accessible

610
00:38:53.025 --> 00:38:56.695
to my constituents and to the world.

611
00:39:00.032 --> 00:39:02.409
In real time!

612
00:39:02.492 --> 00:39:08.123
It will all be on my True You page.
That's right. Thank you, thank you.

613
00:39:08.206 --> 00:39:11.460
Goodbye, backroom deals.
Goodbye, shady lobbyists.

614
00:39:11.543 --> 00:39:16.048
Hello, democracy. Open and accountable!

615
00:39:16.131 --> 00:39:17.716
That's right. That's a fact!

616
00:39:19.426 --> 00:39:22.763
- Where do you work?
- I work in customer experience.

617
00:39:22.846 --> 00:39:26.058
I started in CE, too. A lot of us did.

618
00:39:26.141 --> 00:39:28.060
Cool. Where do you work now?

619
00:39:28.143 --> 00:39:30.979
Biometrics, iris scanning,
facial recognition.

620
00:39:31.063 --> 00:39:33.732
Right now, I'm working on Child Track.

621
00:39:33.815 --> 00:39:36.735
The program protects children from predators.

622
00:39:36.818 --> 00:39:39.696
The second a kid isn't where
they're supposed to be, an alert goes off

623
00:39:39.780 --> 00:39:42.282
and the kid can be tracked down
within 90 seconds.

624
00:39:42.366 --> 00:39:46.578
- Wow. Do they wear a bracelet or...?
- We place chips in the bone.

625
00:39:50.916 --> 00:39:51.917
Uh, you're...

626
00:39:54.419 --> 00:39:55.837
You're serious?

627
00:39:55.921 --> 00:40:00.092
I'm serious about immediately reducing
kidnapping, rape, and murder

628
00:40:00.175 --> 00:40:01.676
by 99 percent.

629
00:40:05.097 --> 00:40:06.306
I think I'm going to get a drink.

630
00:40:06.390 --> 00:40:07.849
- But...
- Oh.

631
00:40:07.933 --> 00:40:10.394
- It was nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

632
00:40:16.817 --> 00:40:18.610
- Hey.
- Hey, check your e-mail

633
00:40:18.693 --> 00:40:22.155
- 'cause I sent you an invite.
- Awesome. Thank you. All right.

634
00:40:22.239 --> 00:40:24.282
- Yeah, good to see you.
- You too.

635
00:40:24.366 --> 00:40:26.034
- Hi, Mae! How's it going?
- Hey.

636
00:40:26.118 --> 00:40:27.702
- You having fun?
- Yeah, you?

637
00:40:27.786 --> 00:40:28.787
Yeah.

638
00:41:01.695 --> 00:41:03.530
- Hey.
- Hey.

639
00:41:07.951 --> 00:41:10.454
So, what did you think
about the whole presentation?

640
00:41:10.537 --> 00:41:12.164
- The Santos thing?
- Yeah.

641
00:41:14.166 --> 00:41:17.294
It's exciting. It's a...

642
00:41:17.377 --> 00:41:21.089
- Momentous moment in the history of...
- Okay, what did you really think?

643
00:41:26.303 --> 00:41:28.430
I thought it was a bit much.

644
00:41:28.513 --> 00:41:29.973
Just a little bit.

645
00:41:32.100 --> 00:41:33.602
So where does all the data go?

646
00:41:33.685 --> 00:41:37.022
Is it stored somewhere? In the Cloud?

647
00:41:38.064 --> 00:41:39.524
There's a physical place, too.

648
00:41:43.195 --> 00:41:44.488
Do you wanna see something?

649
00:42:06.676 --> 00:42:10.597
So you're gonna have to leave
your stuff outside before you go in.

650
00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:13.058
Just because you're not
supposed to... you know.

651
00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:23.485
So I'm just going to have to take... this.
And this.

652
00:42:36.164 --> 00:42:37.249
I want to thank you already for this.

653
00:42:37.332 --> 00:42:40.418
I love following strangers
down dark corridors.

654
00:42:40.502 --> 00:42:43.797
- This is the best.
- Almost there.

655
00:42:50.095 --> 00:42:54.724
This holds Congresswoman Santos,
every second of her from now on.

656
00:42:56.309 --> 00:42:58.144
And this will be the next one.

657
00:43:00.021 --> 00:43:02.941
- Just these?
- Oh, no. There's more.

658
00:43:06.653 --> 00:43:07.779
What is this?

659
00:43:07.862 --> 00:43:11.116
Well, it was supposed to be a part
of a subway but they abandoned it.

660
00:43:12.492 --> 00:43:14.953
- Where does it go?
- It connects to the bay.

661
00:43:15.036 --> 00:43:19.124
But I've traveled about a half a mile
into it, gets too wet.

662
00:43:23.044 --> 00:43:25.005
And that's where the rest of it will be.

663
00:43:26.298 --> 00:43:29.050
- The rest of what?
- The rest of it all.

664
00:43:30.510 --> 00:43:32.596
Santos is just the start.

665
00:43:33.722 --> 00:43:36.975
The plan is everything recorded,
seen, broadcast,

666
00:43:37.058 --> 00:43:41.062
all of it stored, analyzed,
available to the Circle in any way.

667
00:43:42.689 --> 00:43:43.982
You did your health intake, right?

668
00:43:45.317 --> 00:43:46.901
- Yeah.
- See, right? Look.

669
00:43:46.985 --> 00:43:50.363
Every step, every breath, all of it stored.

670
00:43:52.032 --> 00:43:53.742
They can use it however they see fit.

671
00:43:53.825 --> 00:43:55.368
We're being studied.

672
00:43:55.452 --> 00:43:58.997
Everyone's data in the Circle
is being studied, monetized.

673
00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:00.540
It is voluntary.

674
00:44:01.708 --> 00:44:05.003
And isn't that the whole point?
Having everything in one place?

675
00:44:06.671 --> 00:44:08.757
I mean, that's True You.

676
00:44:08.840 --> 00:44:12.469
That's not why I invented True You.
Not even close.

677
00:44:12.552 --> 00:44:14.304
Ty Lafitte invented True You.

678
00:44:15.847 --> 00:44:17.474
Yeah.

679
00:44:17.557 --> 00:44:18.391
I'm Ty.

680
00:44:20.560 --> 00:44:21.770
No, you're not.

681
00:44:21.853 --> 00:44:24.522
Ty Lafitte has been off the grid for years.

682
00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:28.688
Oh, my God.

683
00:44:30.236 --> 00:44:31.071
I'm Ty.

684
00:44:32.072 --> 00:44:33.990
Why the low profile?

685
00:44:34.074 --> 00:44:36.868
I just wanted to move through
this world like a normal person.

686
00:44:36.951 --> 00:44:39.454
I didn't wanna be watched every second.

687
00:44:39.537 --> 00:44:41.706
But I do spend a lot of time here.

688
00:44:41.790 --> 00:44:44.918
It's easier. I don't have to deal
with Stenton and Bailey.

689
00:44:45.001 --> 00:44:49.255
Not that I'm privy to their conversations
in their little sanctum.

690
00:44:49.339 --> 00:44:53.718
Holy shit. I'm in a cave with Ty Lafitte.

691
00:44:53.802 --> 00:44:59.933
Wait, hold on. So when we met,
you didn't know who I was?

692
00:45:00.016 --> 00:45:02.769
You never told me your name.

693
00:45:02.852 --> 00:45:04.896
And after a while, it was just weird to ask.

694
00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:11.027
I knew I could trust you when I met you.
I don't know, I just thought to myself,

695
00:45:11.111 --> 00:45:13.071
this girl, she doesn't have
a cynical bone in her body.

696
00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:15.990
Reminds me of how I used to be.

697
00:45:16.991 --> 00:45:19.661
Listen, you can't tell anyone
that you were here.

698
00:45:19.744 --> 00:45:20.912
Okay? That I brought you here.

699
00:45:21.996 --> 00:45:22.997
Okay.

700
00:45:23.081 --> 00:45:27.419
Things at the Circle, they need to change.

701
00:45:27.502 --> 00:45:29.712
Like what? Connecting the world?

702
00:45:31.005 --> 00:45:32.841
Insuring my parents?

703
00:45:32.924 --> 00:45:35.510
You should be really proud
of what you created.

704
00:45:35.593 --> 00:45:37.595
Look, proud? Proud of what?

705
00:45:37.679 --> 00:45:40.890
The disregard for privacy,
the use of personal data

706
00:45:40.974 --> 00:45:43.435
for the accumulation of wealth and control?

707
00:45:43.518 --> 00:45:45.437
I'm not proud. This is not what I created.

708
00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:47.439
Do you think Senator Williamson
being investigated

709
00:45:47.522 --> 00:45:49.607
by the FBI is just a coincidence?

710
00:45:49.691 --> 00:45:51.526
We can't be seen together,
and you have to promise me.

711
00:45:51.609 --> 00:45:53.236
You can't tell anyone that we were down here.

712
00:46:07.667 --> 00:46:08.751
Hello?

713
00:46:08.835 --> 00:46:11.004
<i> Mae, you have a visitor here to see you.</i>

714
00:46:11.087 --> 00:46:12.255
In the lobby?

715
00:46:18.344 --> 00:46:20.096
- Hey.
- Hey.

716
00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:23.224
- Are you okay?
- No, I'm really not okay.

717
00:46:24.309 --> 00:46:26.478
Why didn't you call, send a text?

718
00:46:26.561 --> 00:46:30.023
Because I wanted to see you in person.
I wanted to look you in the eyes.

719
00:46:31.566 --> 00:46:32.650
Okay.

720
00:46:32.734 --> 00:46:38.490
- Um, I'll sign you in.
- No, just wait, I...

721
00:46:38.573 --> 00:46:42.243
- The stuff you posted about my work.
- Your chandeliers?

722
00:46:42.327 --> 00:46:45.497
Yeah. I know you meant well, but...

723
00:46:45.580 --> 00:46:47.999
What's the problem?
I thought I saw good stuff going up.

724
00:46:48.082 --> 00:46:50.001
They started calling me a deer killer.

725
00:46:51.002 --> 00:46:53.755
And then there's a campaign to shut me down.

726
00:46:53.838 --> 00:46:56.257
I wasn't even online. I was working.

727
00:46:56.341 --> 00:46:59.135
And when I checked my e-mail,
there were death threats.

728
00:47:03.223 --> 00:47:04.265
I'm sorry.

729
00:47:04.349 --> 00:47:05.683
Death threats, Mae.

730
00:47:05.767 --> 00:47:07.602
Obviously, I never meant for this to happen.

731
00:47:07.685 --> 00:47:09.979
But I... I can fix it.

732
00:47:14.359 --> 00:47:16.069
Mae.

733
00:47:16.152 --> 00:47:16.986
Mae.

734
00:47:18.488 --> 00:47:19.572
Will you listen to me?

735
00:47:20.865 --> 00:47:21.741
I can't do this.

736
00:47:21.824 --> 00:47:25.286
I can't be a part of this world
that you're creating.

737
00:47:25.370 --> 00:47:26.496
What are you talking about?

738
00:47:26.579 --> 00:47:28.665
Jesus Christ, look at these people!

739
00:47:28.748 --> 00:47:30.375
Does this really seem okay to you?

740
00:47:30.458 --> 00:47:32.126
Why don't you go kill some more deer, Mercer?

741
00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:35.213
I never killed any deer. I don't kill deer.

742
00:47:35.296 --> 00:47:38.758
Mercer, look, let's not do this here.

743
00:47:38.841 --> 00:47:40.385
Let's go upstairs.

744
00:47:40.468 --> 00:47:42.845
- I'll figure it out.
- Do you ever leave this place, Mae?

745
00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:48.184
We used to go on adventures
and have fun and see things,

746
00:47:48.268 --> 00:47:50.728
and you were brave and excited.

747
00:47:52.564 --> 00:47:54.857
And now it's all filtered through this.

748
00:48:01.322 --> 00:48:02.532
Bye.

749
00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:10.511
I can't believe I was so stupid.

750
00:51:12.513 --> 00:51:14.140
I'm so lucky that you guys were around here.

751
00:51:14.223 --> 00:51:15.892
Luck? Hell.

752
00:51:15.975 --> 00:51:18.394
SeeChange cameras saved your life.

753
00:51:18.478 --> 00:51:19.937
A cop saw you go through the fence,

754
00:51:20.021 --> 00:51:22.398
and then we got a call
from someone watching the seals.

755
00:51:28.863 --> 00:51:30.448
So, Mae, how do you feel?

756
00:51:34.410 --> 00:51:35.369
Ashamed.

757
00:51:35.453 --> 00:51:38.164
I... I barely slept.

758
00:51:38.247 --> 00:51:40.875
Well, I'm just glad you're safe. We all are.

759
00:51:41.918 --> 00:51:44.420
And everyone
who watched your adventure online.

760
00:51:44.504 --> 00:51:46.923
I hope your folks weren't too worried.

761
00:51:47.006 --> 00:51:48.049
They're doing okay.

762
00:51:49.634 --> 00:51:51.761
I understand
your father's condition has improved.

763
00:51:53.095 --> 00:51:54.347
Very much.

764
00:51:58.684 --> 00:51:59.685
Mr. Bailey...

765
00:52:00.686 --> 00:52:03.189
I want to say how much
I value my position here...

766
00:52:05.191 --> 00:52:06.859
and what you've done for my family.

767
00:52:06.943 --> 00:52:10.530
Mae, you're a valued part of the Circle,
and you're family.

768
00:52:10.613 --> 00:52:13.074
And we care about everybody you care about.

769
00:52:14.242 --> 00:52:17.829
You know, this incident
has actually shown a lot of people

770
00:52:17.912 --> 00:52:19.121
the necessity for SeeChange.

771
00:52:20.665 --> 00:52:23.042
I'm so glad those cameras were there.

772
00:52:23.125 --> 00:52:24.544
I wish you would've known
there was a camera there

773
00:52:24.627 --> 00:52:26.879
before you swiped that kayak, but...

774
00:52:29.507 --> 00:52:32.802
Mae, Tom and I want to talk you
about an idea, but before we do,

775
00:52:34.846 --> 00:52:36.556
is there anything you want to tell us?

776
00:52:41.227 --> 00:52:43.437
That I've been here before?

777
00:52:43.521 --> 00:52:46.607
It's only our lies that get us in trouble,
the things we hide.

778
00:52:48.192 --> 00:52:50.611
Of course I know you've been in here before.

779
00:52:52.613 --> 00:52:55.366
And now that I know your secret,
do you feel better or worse?

780
00:53:00.079 --> 00:53:01.956
Better.

781
00:53:02.039 --> 00:53:03.165
Relieved, actually.

782
00:53:05.001 --> 00:53:08.462
I am a believer
in the perfectibility of human beings.

783
00:53:08.546 --> 00:53:12.174
When we are our best selves,
the possibilities are endless.

784
00:53:12.258 --> 00:53:14.302
There isn't a problem that we cannot solve.

785
00:53:14.385 --> 00:53:18.139
We can cure any disease
and we can end hunger, and...

786
00:53:19.849 --> 00:53:24.437
Without secrets, without the hoarding
of knowledge and information,

787
00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:27.315
we can finally realize our potential.

788
00:53:30.359 --> 00:53:32.612
This is a special Dream Friday.

789
00:53:32.695 --> 00:53:37.909
I thought I'd change it up a bit today
and not have a speech but an interview.

790
00:53:39.035 --> 00:53:41.412
I had a conversation
with a young Circler the other day

791
00:53:41.495 --> 00:53:42.997
that I wanted to share with you.

792
00:53:43.998 --> 00:53:47.335
I've asked Mae Holland,
who some of you might know

793
00:53:47.418 --> 00:53:50.922
as one of our new guppies in customer
experience, to join me today.

794
00:53:51.005 --> 00:53:55.176
Mae's only been with us for a few months
but she has made quite an impression.

795
00:53:55.259 --> 00:53:57.595
Let's bring her out. Mae Holland!

796
00:54:08.064 --> 00:54:09.941
Well, hi, Mae, how are you?

797
00:54:10.024 --> 00:54:11.025
- Terrified.
- Mm.

798
00:54:11.108 --> 00:54:12.568
Don't be nervous.

799
00:54:12.652 --> 00:54:14.612
Easy for you to say.

800
00:54:15.905 --> 00:54:17.531
All right, Mae, we have all seen

801
00:54:17.615 --> 00:54:19.325
the SeeChange footage from the other night,

802
00:54:19.408 --> 00:54:21.619
but please describe what happened for us.

803
00:54:23.287 --> 00:54:24.622
I committed a crime.

804
00:54:25.873 --> 00:54:29.377
I borrowed a kayak
without the owner's knowledge,

805
00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:30.836
paddled out to the middle of the bay,

806
00:54:32.338 --> 00:54:35.007
and I wasn't wearing a life jacket.

807
00:54:35.091 --> 00:54:39.428
Well, I'm not sure
that's exactly a capital offense,

808
00:54:39.512 --> 00:54:41.639
but you did risk your own life.

809
00:54:42.890 --> 00:54:45.226
So, Mae, do you think
you behave better or worse

810
00:54:45.309 --> 00:54:48.145
- when you are being watched?
- Better.

811
00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:51.273
Without a doubt.

812
00:54:51.357 --> 00:54:54.110
And what happens when you're alone
and unobserved?

813
00:54:55.194 --> 00:54:57.071
Well, for starters, I steal kayaks.

814
00:54:58.656 --> 00:55:00.908
Jesus. She's a natural.

815
00:55:00.992 --> 00:55:05.121
Seriously. I do things I don't wanna do.

816
00:55:06.414 --> 00:55:07.748
I lie.

817
00:55:07.832 --> 00:55:09.709
When we spoke the other day,
you had a way of putting it

818
00:55:09.792 --> 00:55:11.377
that I thought
was very interesting and succinct.

819
00:55:11.460 --> 00:55:13.170
Could you tell us all what you said?

820
00:55:15.047 --> 00:55:17.174
I said that secrets are lies.

821
00:55:18.384 --> 00:55:19.760
Secrets are lies.

822
00:55:21.721 --> 00:55:22.555
Go on.

823
00:55:23.889 --> 00:55:29.145
Secrets are what make crimes possible.

824
00:55:29.228 --> 00:55:32.023
We behave worse when we're not accountable.

825
00:55:33.441 --> 00:55:35.818
I was my worst self

826
00:55:35.901 --> 00:55:38.487
because I didn't think anyone was watching.

827
00:55:40.114 --> 00:55:40.948
I thought...

828
00:55:42.700 --> 00:55:43.701
that I was alone.

829
00:55:44.827 --> 00:55:48.581
So, what did you see
on this kayaking trip, Mae?

830
00:55:48.664 --> 00:55:50.875
- Was it beautiful?
- It was.

831
00:55:52.043 --> 00:55:53.753
There was almost a full moon.

832
00:55:54.879 --> 00:55:56.130
And it felt like...

833
00:55:58.174 --> 00:56:01.594
it felt like I was paddling
through liquid silver.

834
00:56:01.677 --> 00:56:04.180
Hmm, sounds incredible.

835
00:56:05.264 --> 00:56:07.349
But there's no record of any of it.

836
00:56:07.433 --> 00:56:09.435
- Not outside my own memory.
- Hmm.

837
00:56:12.521 --> 00:56:13.898
Okay.

838
00:56:13.981 --> 00:56:16.442
So now here we get into something personal.

839
00:56:16.525 --> 00:56:20.654
As you know, I have a son, Gunner,

840
00:56:21.739 --> 00:56:24.116
who was born with cerebral palsy.

841
00:56:24.200 --> 00:56:26.285
And though he's living a very full life

842
00:56:26.368 --> 00:56:29.330
and we're always trying
to improve his opportunities,

843
00:56:29.413 --> 00:56:32.458
he is confined to a wheelchair.

844
00:56:32.541 --> 00:56:35.544
He can't walk.

845
00:56:35.628 --> 00:56:37.546
He can't run.

846
00:56:37.630 --> 00:56:39.799
He can't go kayaking.

847
00:56:41.008 --> 00:56:44.553
So, what does he do if he wants
to experience something like that?

848
00:56:44.637 --> 00:56:46.639
Well, he watches video.

849
00:56:46.722 --> 00:56:48.182
He looks at pictures.

850
00:56:49.391 --> 00:56:53.521
And much of his experiences of the world
come through the experience of others.

851
00:56:53.604 --> 00:56:57.233
When he experiences the view
of a Circler climbing Mount Kenya,

852
00:56:57.316 --> 00:56:59.443
he feels like he's climbed Mount Kenya.

853
00:57:01.654 --> 00:57:05.116
When he sees firsthand video
from an America's Cup crew member,

854
00:57:05.199 --> 00:57:10.287
he feels in some way that he's sailed
in the America's Cup too.

855
00:57:10.371 --> 00:57:13.749
And these experiences
were facilitated by generous humans

856
00:57:13.833 --> 00:57:16.919
who shared what they saw with the world,
my son included,

857
00:57:17.002 --> 00:57:19.421
and there are many others out there
that are just like Gunner.

858
00:57:19.505 --> 00:57:22.133
Now does it feel right to have deprived them

859
00:57:22.216 --> 00:57:23.926
of seeing what you saw, Mae?

860
00:57:27.596 --> 00:57:28.597
It doesn't.

861
00:57:31.767 --> 00:57:32.935
It feels very wrong.

862
00:57:35.896 --> 00:57:37.022
It was selfish.

863
00:57:38.899 --> 00:57:44.238
When you deprive others of experiences
like the ones I had,

864
00:57:47.116 --> 00:57:49.118
you're essentially stealing from them.

865
00:57:50.161 --> 00:57:54.582
Knowledge is a basic human right.

866
00:57:57.042 --> 00:58:03.424
Access to all possible human experience
is a basic human right.

867
00:58:16.812 --> 00:58:20.357
Thank you, Mae, for your candor
and your consummate humanity.

868
00:58:20.441 --> 00:58:22.526
Now... now the best part.

869
00:58:22.610 --> 00:58:25.237
In the interest of sharing
everything she can with the world,

870
00:58:25.321 --> 00:58:28.032
Mae has, uh... Mae has some news.

871
00:58:31.660 --> 00:58:32.828
From now on...

872
00:58:34.747 --> 00:58:41.462
I'll be wearing a modified
SeeChange camera at all times.

873
00:58:43.964 --> 00:58:46.175
I'm going fully transparent.

874
00:58:49.178 --> 00:58:51.222
Well, maybe not in the bathroom.

875
00:58:53.265 --> 00:58:56.769
I'll be starting immediately.

876
00:58:56.852 --> 00:58:57.686
That's right.

877
00:58:59.647 --> 00:59:03.234
Mae will be the first Circler
to share every single second

878
00:59:03.317 --> 00:59:06.445
of her work
and her personal life with the public,

879
00:59:06.529 --> 00:59:08.822
so let's give her an even bigger hand!

880
00:59:18.874 --> 00:59:20.125
I'm proud of you.

881
00:59:25.756 --> 00:59:28.551
Mae! Mae! Mae! Mae!

882
00:59:28.634 --> 00:59:29.927
Mae! Mae! Mae! Mae!

883
00:59:48.571 --> 00:59:49.863
<i>Hello.</i>

884
00:59:49.947 --> 00:59:51.907
Good morning, everyone.

885
00:59:51.991 --> 00:59:55.202
<i>The sun is shining,</i>
<i>and I definitely need my coffee.</i>

886
00:59:56.412 --> 00:59:59.373
We are in my third week of transparency,

887
00:59:59.456 --> 01:00:01.542
and we're up to...

888
01:00:01.625 --> 01:00:05.713
2,308,007 viewers.

889
01:00:05.796 --> 01:00:06.630
<i>Wow.</i>

890
01:00:06.714 --> 01:00:08.882
Did I tell you guys I'm not a morning person?

891
01:00:10.050 --> 01:00:13.762
Okay, among all of those viewers...

892
01:00:14.888 --> 01:00:15.723
are...

893
01:00:16.724 --> 01:00:19.101
- <i>I don't even know what...</i>
- Hi, Mom, hi, Dad.

894
01:00:19.184 --> 01:00:21.020
- <i>Hi!</i>
- <i>Hi, Mae.</i>

895
01:00:21.103 --> 01:00:22.605
Say hello to everyone.

896
01:00:22.688 --> 01:00:24.732
<i>Hello, Circle.</i>

897
01:00:24.815 --> 01:00:27.318
Look. We match.

898
01:00:27.401 --> 01:00:28.986
- <i>Yeah.</i>
- <i>We sure do.</i>

899
01:00:29.069 --> 01:00:31.530
- <i>These are great.</i>
- <i>Very stylish.</i>

900
01:00:31.614 --> 01:00:33.782
I love you guys. I'll see you later.

901
01:00:33.866 --> 01:00:36.160
- <i>Love you. Bye.</i>
- <i>Love you too, sweetheart.</i>

902
01:00:41.582 --> 01:00:45.377
- Hello, Circlers.
- <i>Hi, Mae.</i>

903
01:00:45.461 --> 01:00:48.464
So this is where I worked
when I first arrived at the Circle,

904
01:00:48.547 --> 01:00:51.925
and this is where I still work at the Circle.

905
01:00:52.009 --> 01:00:55.320
Because even though I have you guys
looking over my shoulder...

906
01:00:55.403 --> 01:00:58.098
I'm still basically a guppy.

907
01:00:58.182 --> 01:01:02.936
- Go! Do it!
- Do it! Do it! Do it!

908
01:01:03.897 --> 01:01:05.898
<i>Oh, my God! </i>

909
01:01:05.981 --> 01:01:08.108
<i>What are you doing?</i>
<i>Tell your own stories, sheep.</i>

910
01:01:08.192 --> 01:01:10.569
<i>Stop looking at us, sheep!</i>

911
01:01:10.653 --> 01:01:12.279
<i>All right, that's our scene.</i>

912
01:01:15.199 --> 01:01:19.620
Sorry about that. Okay, so, next up,
we just need the name of a person

913
01:01:19.703 --> 01:01:23.540
who is historically significant.

914
01:01:23.624 --> 01:01:26.710
- Mae Holland!
- Oh, my God!

915
01:01:26.794 --> 01:01:28.295
<i>Sorry, we don't know who that is.</i>

916
01:01:28.379 --> 01:01:30.757
She's nobody, trust me.

917
01:01:34.009 --> 01:01:35.678
Home sweet home.

918
01:01:35.761 --> 01:01:38.013
Speaking of which,
let's check in with the folks.

919
01:01:40.349 --> 01:01:42.601
Hey, where are you guys?

920
01:01:47.481 --> 01:01:48.482
<i>Yeah.</i>

921
01:01:48.565 --> 01:01:52.656
- <i>Yeah. There you go.</i>
- Whoa! Ah! Um...

922
01:01:53.028 --> 01:01:55.239
Uh, I hope the engineers are watching,

923
01:01:55.322 --> 01:01:57.574
because that definitely
shouldn't have happened...

924
01:01:57.658 --> 01:02:00.994
and, um, it definitely won't happen again.

925
01:02:01.078 --> 01:02:02.705
You can trust me there.

926
01:02:13.298 --> 01:02:15.676
<i>This is me brushing my teeth.</i>

927
01:02:15.759 --> 01:02:17.845
<i>If you're enjoying this,</i>
<i>I'm worried about you.</i>

928
01:02:34.319 --> 01:02:35.779
Good night, everyone.

929
01:02:36.989 --> 01:02:39.533
I'm not gonna lie, it was a crazy day,

930
01:02:39.616 --> 01:02:42.703
but I'm happy I had you with me,
each and every one of you.

931
01:03:32.753 --> 01:03:34.254
<i>Never a boring day here.</i>

932
01:03:35.422 --> 01:03:38.550
A lot of you guys have been asking me
about this sculpture,

933
01:03:38.634 --> 01:03:39.927
which is a treat for me.

934
01:03:40.010 --> 01:03:42.721
I finally get to use my art history degree.

935
01:03:42.805 --> 01:03:45.682
<i>This was made by an artist</i>
<i>who has frequently been in trouble</i>

936
01:03:45.766 --> 01:03:47.267
<i>in his own country.</i>

937
01:03:47.351 --> 01:03:51.355
I wanna thank all the watchers
who sent frowns to the government there,

938
01:03:51.438 --> 01:03:54.399
both for their persecution of the artist

939
01:03:54.483 --> 01:03:57.152
and for their restriction
on Internet freedoms.

940
01:03:57.236 --> 01:04:01.615
We have sent 180 million frowns
from the U.S. alone.

941
01:04:01.698 --> 01:04:04.827
You can bet that's gonna have
an effect on the regime.

942
01:04:07.412 --> 01:04:08.622
Annie.

943
01:04:11.416 --> 01:04:12.417
<i>Annie.</i>

944
01:04:13.919 --> 01:04:14.920
Annie!

945
01:04:18.340 --> 01:04:19.174
Annie!

946
01:04:26.682 --> 01:04:27.724
Annie!

947
01:04:29.768 --> 01:04:31.183
Annie Allerton!

948
01:04:43.365 --> 01:04:44.199
Are we alone?

949
01:04:45.951 --> 01:04:49.846
I have three minutes to use
the bathroom without the video on.

950
01:04:50.029 --> 01:04:51.447
Pervs.

951
01:04:52.875 --> 01:04:54.042
How are you?

952
01:04:56.336 --> 01:04:58.046
- Good.
- Well...

953
01:04:58.130 --> 01:04:59.131
you should be good.

954
01:04:59.214 --> 01:05:00.215
You're killing it.

955
01:05:01.466 --> 01:05:02.301
Do you think?

956
01:05:02.384 --> 01:05:04.344
Oh, come on, false modesty won't work here.

957
01:05:04.428 --> 01:05:05.304
You should be psyched.

958
01:05:06.638 --> 01:05:07.472
Yeah.

959
01:05:08.724 --> 01:05:10.767
- I am.
- You're a meteor. It's insane.

960
01:05:10.851 --> 01:05:14.262
People are coming to me trying
to get to you. It's... crazy.

961
01:05:15.898 --> 01:05:17.107
How are you really, Annie?

962
01:05:18.483 --> 01:05:19.568
You look wiped.

963
01:05:19.651 --> 01:05:22.654
Thanks, Mae.
You know how much I like to be told

964
01:05:22.738 --> 01:05:24.489
right after I appear
in front of your millions

965
01:05:24.573 --> 01:05:27.200
that I look terrible.
Thank you, you're sweet.

966
01:05:27.284 --> 01:05:28.577
Let me take you for dinner.

967
01:05:30.954 --> 01:05:32.497
You gotta eat sometime.

968
01:05:32.581 --> 01:05:36.043
Your camera and me looking so terrible.
That sounds fantastic, but, no.

969
01:05:36.126 --> 01:05:38.712
I just... I'm worried about you.

970
01:05:38.795 --> 01:05:41.882
- You're working too hard.
- You're worried about me?

971
01:05:41.965 --> 01:05:43.717
You're... I'm worried about the EU,

972
01:05:43.800 --> 01:05:45.177
but it's good to know
you're worried about me.

973
01:05:45.260 --> 01:05:47.721
- I didn't mean it that way.
- Don't worry in any way.

974
01:05:51.099 --> 01:05:54.019
- I know you can handle it.
- Oh, thank you, Mae.

975
01:05:54.102 --> 01:05:56.688
Your confidence in me
will be the wind beneath my wings.

976
01:05:58.649 --> 01:05:59.608
How are your parents?

977
01:06:03.236 --> 01:06:04.237
Sorry.

978
01:06:06.698 --> 01:06:09.493
They're over that little... episode?

979
01:06:13.705 --> 01:06:16.375
I haven't heard from them, actually.

980
01:06:18.293 --> 01:06:20.420
I guess it'll just take some time.

981
01:06:20.504 --> 01:06:22.631
Sorry, Mae. Shit.

982
01:06:24.800 --> 01:06:25.801
I'm fried.

983
01:06:27.970 --> 01:06:29.680
And I have to pull another all-nighter.

984
01:06:31.264 --> 01:06:33.475
Stenton is on my ass all the time.

985
01:06:33.558 --> 01:06:37.020
I shouldn't have questioned him
about that whole Senator Williamson thing.

986
01:06:38.105 --> 01:06:41.733
And then...
there's all the new work on Completion.

987
01:06:42.818 --> 01:06:44.653
But I assume Bailey told you about that.

988
01:06:47.698 --> 01:06:49.408
Of course.

989
01:06:49.491 --> 01:06:50.784
Of course.

990
01:06:50.867 --> 01:06:52.995
I'll be there
at the Concept Kingdom meeting tomorrow.

991
01:06:53.078 --> 01:06:53.912
What?

992
01:06:56.081 --> 01:06:58.917
- There's a Concept Kingdom...
- I know what it is. But you're going?

993
01:06:59.001 --> 01:07:03.797
- Yeah. Bailey said I should be there.
- And broadcast it?

994
01:07:05.674 --> 01:07:07.217
- Is that a problem?
- No.

995
01:07:07.300 --> 01:07:10.012
I'm just surprised, that's all.
Those meetings are sensitive.

996
01:07:10.095 --> 01:07:13.181
You know, maybe he's planning on
having you attend just the beginning.

997
01:07:13.265 --> 01:07:15.517
I mean, Mae, you're in over your head.

998
01:07:18.103 --> 01:07:19.646
Are you leaving?

999
01:07:19.730 --> 01:07:21.064
Yeah.

1000
01:07:22.149 --> 01:07:24.735
I'm really so far behind on everything,
I could just die.

1001
01:07:28.697 --> 01:07:29.698
Annie?

1002
01:07:31.716 --> 01:07:33.001
<i>Hello.</i>

1003
01:07:34.703 --> 01:07:35.537
See ya.

1004
01:07:45.964 --> 01:07:50.385
<i>So here's how many people voted</i>
<i>in the last national election.</i>

1005
01:07:50.469 --> 01:07:53.430
<i>Here's how many were eligible to vote.</i>

1006
01:07:55.557 --> 01:07:59.770
And here is how many Americans
are registered in the Circle.

1007
01:08:01.354 --> 01:08:03.190
And there's that guy.

1008
01:08:03.273 --> 01:08:05.400
So now we get down to the meat
of today's session,

1009
01:08:05.484 --> 01:08:08.612
something we've discussed
with Congresswoman Santos and others.

1010
01:08:09.988 --> 01:08:16.411
What if... your Circle profile
automatically registered you to vote?

1011
01:08:18.246 --> 01:08:20.582
With True You, in order to set up a profile,

1012
01:08:20.665 --> 01:08:23.335
you have to be a real person
with a real address,

1013
01:08:23.418 --> 01:08:27.047
the complete personal info,
real social security number,

1014
01:08:27.130 --> 01:08:29.257
with all the information
that the government wants

1015
01:08:29.341 --> 01:08:31.301
when you register to vote.

1016
01:08:31.384 --> 01:08:35.430
So why wouldn't the state
consider you already registered

1017
01:08:35.514 --> 01:08:37.140
when you open a Circle account?

1018
01:08:38.225 --> 01:08:39.810
There's no reason.

1019
01:08:39.893 --> 01:08:44.856
Uh, we've got verbal commitments
from governors of almost every state,

1020
01:08:44.940 --> 01:08:49.945
and they've agreed to push for legislation
that would make your Circle account

1021
01:08:50.028 --> 01:08:52.572
an automatic path to voter registration.

1022
01:08:54.825 --> 01:08:55.659
Bravo.

1023
01:08:59.412 --> 01:09:00.872
I think it's a great idea.

1024
01:09:06.211 --> 01:09:08.672
Well, yes, Mae.

1025
01:09:12.342 --> 01:09:14.594
Why couldn't we take it one step further?

1026
01:09:15.595 --> 01:09:16.972
I mean, I...

1027
01:09:17.055 --> 01:09:20.642
I don't have it completely figured out yet...

1028
01:09:20.725 --> 01:09:23.854
No, no, no, go on, go on, go on.
You started really well there.

1029
01:09:23.937 --> 01:09:26.898
I like those words, "one step further."
It's how the company was built.

1030
01:09:29.067 --> 01:09:33.029
Okay. Working backwards from your goal...

1031
01:09:33.113 --> 01:09:34.698
Go on.

1032
01:09:34.781 --> 01:09:37.659
We all agree 100 percent participation
is what we'd like.

1033
01:09:37.742 --> 01:09:40.245
A hundred percent participation is the ideal.

1034
01:09:40.328 --> 01:09:43.498
That's the idealist's ideal, yes.

1035
01:09:43.582 --> 01:09:47.961
And we already have 83 percent
of voting-age Americans

1036
01:09:48.044 --> 01:09:49.462
registered to the Circle.

1037
01:09:49.546 --> 01:09:50.755
Yep.

1038
01:09:50.839 --> 01:09:52.674
And it seems we're on our way

1039
01:09:52.757 --> 01:09:56.970
to users being able to register it
and maybe actually vote

1040
01:09:57.053 --> 01:09:58.054
through their Circle account.

1041
01:09:58.138 --> 01:10:02.684
Well, that's a small leap, but... Yes.
Okay, go on.

1042
01:10:04.811 --> 01:10:09.941
Why not require every voting-age citizen
to have a Circle account?

1043
01:10:11.025 --> 01:10:12.861
Let her finish. Let her finish.

1044
01:10:12.944 --> 01:10:16.323
Okay, I know the initial reaction
will be resistance.

1045
01:10:16.406 --> 01:10:19.826
I mean, how can we require anyone
to use our services?

1046
01:10:20.911 --> 01:10:22.787
But there are all kinds of things
that are mandatory

1047
01:10:22.871 --> 01:10:23.955
for citizens of this country.

1048
01:10:24.039 --> 01:10:25.999
Yep. We have to pay taxes we don't wanna pay.

1049
01:10:26.082 --> 01:10:27.709
And we have to pay for Social Security.

1050
01:10:27.792 --> 01:10:30.462
- We have to serve on juries.
- Right.

1051
01:10:30.545 --> 01:10:32.964
And we have to pee indoors,
not on the street.

1052
01:10:33.048 --> 01:10:35.342
- We do. We do have to be pee indoors.
- She's right.

1053
01:10:35.425 --> 01:10:37.636
I mean, we have thousands of laws.

1054
01:10:37.719 --> 01:10:40.889
We require citizens to do hundreds of things,

1055
01:10:40.972 --> 01:10:43.683
but we don't require them to vote?

1056
01:10:45.560 --> 01:10:48.980
They are already doing this
in dozens of countries.

1057
01:10:49.064 --> 01:10:53.485
With the technology we already have,
users will already be registered.

1058
01:10:53.568 --> 01:10:58.698
That means you have 241 million voters
eligible to vote.

1059
01:10:58.782 --> 01:11:03.328
You have 241 million voters
that have to vote.

1060
01:11:05.872 --> 01:11:07.791
You have the full will of the nation.

1061
01:11:07.874 --> 01:11:10.543
- And how exactly do we do that?
- Annie.

1062
01:11:11.795 --> 01:11:16.299
Well, you know,
it could be something as simple as a zing,

1063
01:11:16.383 --> 01:11:20.720
"Hey, Annie.
Remember to take five minutes and vote."

1064
01:11:20.804 --> 01:11:25.016
It's what we would do
with our own surveys. You know that.

1065
01:11:25.100 --> 01:11:29.437
- But, Mae, what about stragglers?
- We all pay taxes.

1066
01:11:31.064 --> 01:11:33.441
And what, 80 percent of us
did that online last year.

1067
01:11:35.819 --> 01:11:38.613
Why not stop duplicating services?

1068
01:11:39.781 --> 01:11:43.076
Why not just make it all part
of one unified system?

1069
01:11:43.159 --> 01:11:48.039
You could pay your taxes,
vote, pay your parking ticket,

1070
01:11:48.123 --> 01:11:50.333
all through your Circle account.

1071
01:11:50.417 --> 01:11:53.128
We'd save users hundreds of hours
of inconvenience.

1072
01:11:53.211 --> 01:11:56.464
- We'd save the government billions.
- Hundreds of billions.

1073
01:11:56.548 --> 01:11:58.758
We could eliminate half of it overnight.

1074
01:11:58.842 --> 01:12:01.094
Why wouldn't the government
just build a similar service?

1075
01:12:01.177 --> 01:12:02.470
Why the hell does it need us?

1076
01:12:05.515 --> 01:12:08.518
It would cost too much.
They don't have the expertise.

1077
01:12:08.601 --> 01:12:11.980
- We already have the infrastructure.
- You're absolutely right.

1078
01:12:12.063 --> 01:12:16.401
The government needs us
more than we need them.

1079
01:12:16.484 --> 01:12:20.780
Imagine having the full will of the people.

1080
01:12:22.365 --> 01:12:23.408
Instantly.

1081
01:12:25.660 --> 01:12:29.039
You'd have... true democracy

1082
01:12:30.123 --> 01:12:32.375
for the first time in human history.

1083
01:12:33.752 --> 01:12:35.337
This is such bullshit.

1084
01:12:40.675 --> 01:12:43.178
No voter suppression.
You're voting from home.

1085
01:12:43.261 --> 01:12:45.930
<i>Think of the implications</i>
<i>for totalitarian regimes.</i>

1086
01:12:46.014 --> 01:12:47.599
No more rigged elections.

1087
01:12:47.682 --> 01:12:49.726
The UN can demand
that they're held through the Circle.

1088
01:12:49.809 --> 01:12:52.812
But only if everyone is heard.

1089
01:12:52.896 --> 01:12:55.648
Every human on Earth.

1090
01:12:55.732 --> 01:12:57.734
We can take the pulse of the nation,

1091
01:12:57.817 --> 01:13:01.321
<i>everyone in the world in seconds</i>
<i>on any subject.</i>

1092
01:13:01.404 --> 01:13:03.573
- It's the only way, right?
- Right.

1093
01:13:03.656 --> 01:13:04.908
Yeah!

1094
01:13:07.660 --> 01:13:11.748
So it looks like I've got 20 minutes here.

1095
01:13:11.831 --> 01:13:14.292
Let's try Mom and Dad.

1096
01:13:17.878 --> 01:13:20.164
- Hey, Mae, what's up?
- Hi.

1097
01:13:25.678 --> 01:13:28.181
- Hey, where have you been?
- <i>Mae.</i>

1098
01:13:28.264 --> 01:13:31.434
I've seen your cameras aren't working.
I can send Tech out...

1099
01:13:31.518 --> 01:13:34.062
<i>Yeah, we disconnected them.</i>

1100
01:13:34.145 --> 01:13:35.688
<i>Sweetheart...</i>

1101
01:13:36.856 --> 01:13:40.235
<i>We're sorry, but we,</i>
<i>we can't keep doing this.</i>

1102
01:13:40.318 --> 01:13:44.030
<i>It's... Well, it's more than we ever...</i>

1103
01:13:44.114 --> 01:13:47.200
<i>You know we love you, and we don't wanna...</i>

1104
01:13:47.283 --> 01:13:51.538
- <i>We love you. We love you.</i>
- <i>We have to have our privacy.</i>

1105
01:13:51.621 --> 01:13:55.125
<i>And we're worried you don't realize</i>
<i>what you've gotten yourself into.</i>

1106
01:13:55.208 --> 01:13:56.251
I'm so sorry.

1107
01:13:57.377 --> 01:13:58.670
I didn't mean to hurt you.

1108
01:13:58.753 --> 01:14:00.630
- <i>It's not you. It's us.</i>
- <i>No, we know that.</i>

1109
01:14:00.713 --> 01:14:03.675
- <i>Yeah, it's us.</i>
- <i>We just need you to understand that.</i>

1110
01:14:06.511 --> 01:14:09.639
- I love you.
- <i>We love you too.</i>

1111
01:14:09.722 --> 01:14:10.557
<i>Bye, honey.</i>

1112
01:14:14.018 --> 01:14:16.771
- You're the one, Mae!
- You're changing the world, Mae!

1113
01:14:34.831 --> 01:14:38.544
Hello, everyone. I'm Mae Holland.

1114
01:14:45.006 --> 01:14:49.846
Okay. We've had some good weeks
thinking about Completion,

1115
01:14:49.929 --> 01:14:53.099
envisioning a day when everyone
is in the Circle,

1116
01:14:53.183 --> 01:14:57.145
when all of humanity is connected and united.

1117
01:14:58.229 --> 01:14:59.898
And it will happen.

1118
01:14:59.981 --> 01:15:02.442
Already, 22 nations have agreed in principle

1119
01:15:02.525 --> 01:15:05.778
to operate their elections through the Circle

1120
01:15:05.862 --> 01:15:10.700
with mandatory participation
from all of its citizens.

1121
01:15:16.664 --> 01:15:22.212
And when everyone is united,
everyone is known, heard, accountable,

1122
01:15:23.463 --> 01:15:25.131
they can be better served.

1123
01:15:25.215 --> 01:15:29.677
But what about the people
who aren't part of the Circle,

1124
01:15:29.761 --> 01:15:33.181
who don't want to be woven
into the fabric of society

1125
01:15:33.264 --> 01:15:36.893
or worse, want to tear out that fabric?

1126
01:15:37.894 --> 01:15:42.106
I want to introduce Soul Search.

1127
01:15:42.190 --> 01:15:46.152
And we're putting it to the test
in front of the entire Circle audience,

1128
01:15:46.236 --> 01:15:49.531
here and globally.

1129
01:15:49.614 --> 01:15:50.615
Do you feel ready?

1130
01:15:50.698 --> 01:15:52.742
Hell, yeah!

1131
01:15:52.825 --> 01:15:54.410
<i>You went well so far.</i>

1132
01:15:54.494 --> 01:15:55.578
<i>Great.</i>

1133
01:15:58.122 --> 01:16:03.211
In seconds, the computer will select,
at random, a fugitive from justice,

1134
01:16:03.294 --> 01:16:06.714
a proven menace to our global community.

1135
01:16:07.882 --> 01:16:10.885
Our assertion is that whoever he or she is,

1136
01:16:10.969 --> 01:16:15.306
Soul Search will locate them
in under 20 minutes.

1137
01:16:15.390 --> 01:16:17.267
Wow.

1138
01:16:20.645 --> 01:16:22.272
Let's select that fugitive.

1139
01:16:24.023 --> 01:16:25.608
Okay.

1140
01:16:25.692 --> 01:16:28.278
This is Fiona Highbridge,

1141
01:16:28.361 --> 01:16:31.281
44 years old born in Manchester, England.

1142
01:16:31.364 --> 01:16:34.659
She was convicted of triple murder in 2004.

1143
01:16:34.742 --> 01:16:37.161
She locked her three children in the closet

1144
01:16:38.204 --> 01:16:40.498
and went on holiday to Spain.

1145
01:16:42.792 --> 01:16:44.043
They all starved.

1146
01:16:45.837 --> 01:16:48.089
She was sent to prison in England but escaped

1147
01:16:48.172 --> 01:16:51.217
with the help of a guard
who she apparently seduced.

1148
01:16:52.885 --> 01:16:57.223
Over a billion people are watching.

1149
01:16:57.307 --> 01:16:59.183
Let's see how many are in the UK.

1150
01:17:00.768 --> 01:17:03.479
Do you all think 14 million Brits

1151
01:17:03.563 --> 01:17:06.274
and a billion global participants worldwide

1152
01:17:06.357 --> 01:17:09.569
can find Fiona Highbridge
in under 20 minutes?

1153
01:17:09.652 --> 01:17:10.820
Yeah!

1154
01:17:12.363 --> 01:17:14.282
Yeah!

1155
01:17:14.365 --> 01:17:16.409
All right.

1156
01:17:16.492 --> 01:17:18.077
Let's bring up the clock.

1157
01:17:19.078 --> 01:17:20.788
Good.

1158
01:17:20.872 --> 01:17:22.950
Is everyone ready?

1159
01:17:24.334 --> 01:17:25.710
On your mark...

1160
01:17:27.378 --> 01:17:28.463
get set...

1161
01:17:29.797 --> 01:17:30.632
go!

1162
01:17:38.640 --> 01:17:40.808
Okay, time to get serious.

1163
01:18:10.254 --> 01:18:11.339
<i>I've got her, Mae.</i>

1164
01:18:12.882 --> 01:18:13.800
<i>That's her.</i>

1165
01:18:14.801 --> 01:18:16.761
Be careful.

1166
01:18:16.844 --> 01:18:17.679
<i>I will.</i>

1167
01:18:39.075 --> 01:18:40.660
<i>Fiona! Stop!</i>

1168
01:18:41.744 --> 01:18:44.789
- Follow her.
- <i>Stop her!</i>

1169
01:18:44.872 --> 01:18:46.499
<i>Somebody stop her! She's a killer!</i>

1170
01:18:47.792 --> 01:18:48.793
<i>Stop her!</i>

1171
01:18:49.794 --> 01:18:51.087
<i>Somebody stop her!</i>

1172
01:18:52.588 --> 01:18:54.298
- Go!
- Grab her!

1173
01:18:57.427 --> 01:18:59.303
<i>Stop her!</i>

1174
01:18:59.387 --> 01:19:01.514
<i>Somebody grab her!</i>

1175
01:19:01.597 --> 01:19:04.100
- <i>She must be kept safe.</i>
- Keep her safe.

1176
01:19:04.183 --> 01:19:07.437
- <i>It's Fiona Highbridge!</i>
- <i>No! No! No!</i>

1177
01:19:19.699 --> 01:19:20.783
<i>All right. Watch it.</i>

1178
01:19:23.453 --> 01:19:24.287
<i>No.</i>

1179
01:19:26.080 --> 01:19:26.914
Stop the clock.

1180
01:19:29.667 --> 01:19:30.960
Soul Search, baby!

1181
01:19:31.043 --> 01:19:33.504
I guess that's it.

1182
01:19:43.222 --> 01:19:45.641
Let's do another!

1183
01:19:45.725 --> 01:19:46.726
We could.

1184
01:19:48.060 --> 01:19:49.812
- Um...
- <i>Maybe not a fugitive this time.</i>

1185
01:19:49.896 --> 01:19:51.856
Let's try for a regular civilian.

1186
01:19:53.316 --> 01:19:55.151
Okay, everyone.

1187
01:19:55.234 --> 01:19:58.029
We're gonna find a regular person.

1188
01:19:59.030 --> 01:20:01.157
It can be anyone.

1189
01:20:01.240 --> 01:20:04.118
A relative, a lost love.

1190
01:20:04.202 --> 01:20:08.998
When everyone can be found,
we can never lose a friend.

1191
01:20:10.958 --> 01:20:12.794
We can take a suggestion from the audience.

1192
01:20:12.877 --> 01:20:16.881
- Uh... anyone?
- Find Mercer!

1193
01:20:20.009 --> 01:20:22.428
I'm pretty sure he doesn't wanna be found.

1194
01:20:23.805 --> 01:20:25.473
- Anyone else?
- Where's Mercer?

1195
01:20:25.556 --> 01:20:29.852
<i>No, Mae, this is what Soul Search</i>
<i>is all about as well as Completion.</i>

1196
01:20:29.936 --> 01:20:30.937
Other suggestions?

1197
01:20:31.020 --> 01:20:32.814
- Let's try Mercer.
- Mercer! Mercer! Mercer!

1198
01:20:32.897 --> 01:20:33.856
<i>It's the whole point of Soul Search, Mae.</i>

1199
01:20:33.940 --> 01:20:34.774
No.

1200
01:20:34.857 --> 01:20:37.068
Mercer! Mercer!

1201
01:20:37.151 --> 01:20:40.738
- Mercer! Mercer! Mercer!
- No, no, no, no. No, I...

1202
01:20:40.822 --> 01:20:43.241
<i>Mae, this is the right choice.</i>

1203
01:20:43.324 --> 01:20:45.618
Wait. Hey, everybody.

1204
01:20:45.701 --> 01:20:46.994
Hey.

1205
01:20:48.120 --> 01:20:50.456
I think it's time we reunited
Mae and Mercer, don't you?

1206
01:20:51.457 --> 01:20:53.668
All right then.
And through the power of the Circle,

1207
01:20:53.751 --> 01:20:56.254
let's bring them back together again, okay?

1208
01:20:56.337 --> 01:21:00.091
The perfect use of Soul Search.
Let's find your friend.

1209
01:21:02.677 --> 01:21:03.928
Go, Mae, now.

1210
01:21:14.438 --> 01:21:16.899
Okay.

1211
01:21:19.944 --> 01:21:26.117
So our second Soul Search today
involves not a fugitive from justice,

1212
01:21:26.200 --> 01:21:28.619
but you might say a fugitive from, um...

1213
01:21:29.954 --> 01:21:30.788
a friendship.

1214
01:21:39.046 --> 01:21:40.882
This is my friend Mercer.

1215
01:21:41.924 --> 01:21:44.510
I haven't seen him in a while.

1216
01:21:44.594 --> 01:21:45.887
I'd love to see him again.

1217
01:21:47.054 --> 01:21:52.268
Like Fiona, though, he is someone
who is trying not to be found,

1218
01:21:52.351 --> 01:21:54.353
but unlike Fiona, he's not a criminal.

1219
01:21:55.897 --> 01:21:57.064
He's good, people.

1220
01:22:04.864 --> 01:22:06.657
Let's see if we can break
our previous record.

1221
01:22:06.741 --> 01:22:08.409
Whoo!

1222
01:22:29.563 --> 01:22:30.973
Oh, yeah.

1223
01:22:31.974 --> 01:22:33.351
Unbelievable.

1224
01:22:33.434 --> 01:22:35.353
Can you believe that? Yes!

1225
01:22:37.563 --> 01:22:39.106
<i>Do you think he's in here?</i>

1226
01:22:41.901 --> 01:22:43.694
- <i>Mercer!</i>
- <i>Mercer!</i>

1227
01:22:43.778 --> 01:22:44.695
<i>Mercer, are you in there?</i>

1228
01:22:44.779 --> 01:22:47.448
<i>Are you, like, killing deer,</i>
<i>making chandeliers?</i>

1229
01:22:49.367 --> 01:22:52.161
<i>Mercer, we know you're in there.</i>
<i>We see your truck. It's right here.</i>

1230
01:22:52.244 --> 01:22:54.038
- <i>I think he's in there.</i>
- <i>Fine. Let's go around.</i>

1231
01:22:54.121 --> 01:22:57.375
<i>Oh, my God, he's in there!</i>

1232
01:22:57.458 --> 01:22:59.335
<i>Mercer, come out. He's gotta be here.</i>

1233
01:22:59.418 --> 01:23:01.629
Remember to be nice, okay?

1234
01:23:01.712 --> 01:23:03.005
<i>Mercer, we know you're in there.</i>

1235
01:23:03.089 --> 01:23:04.924
You found where he is. You did it.

1236
01:23:05.007 --> 01:23:06.258
I think we should leave.

1237
01:23:06.342 --> 01:23:08.970
<i>No, Mae, stay on this.</i>

1238
01:23:09.053 --> 01:23:11.138
<i>You've got millions of people enjoying this.</i>

1239
01:23:11.222 --> 01:23:12.890
<i>There he is. There he is.</i>

1240
01:23:12.974 --> 01:23:15.726
<i>There he is. We just got Mercer.</i>
<i>I cannot believe this.</i>

1241
01:23:15.810 --> 01:23:17.520
<i>There he is! He's in the driveway!</i>

1242
01:23:24.402 --> 01:23:26.487
<i>Leave me alone! Jesus Christ!</i>

1243
01:23:47.925 --> 01:23:49.343
You're kidding me.

1244
01:23:53.764 --> 01:23:56.350
Can someone please get me audio
on the camera?

1245
01:23:58.060 --> 01:23:59.478
Mercer.

1246
01:23:59.562 --> 01:24:02.523
Mercer, stop driving. It's just me.

1247
01:24:02.606 --> 01:24:04.233
Mercer, stop the truck.

1248
01:24:04.316 --> 01:24:06.444
You're surrounded by friends.

1249
01:24:07.820 --> 01:24:09.572
Can we turn the speaker up?

1250
01:24:10.823 --> 01:24:11.824
Mercer.

1251
01:24:13.325 --> 01:24:14.493
Can you hear me?

1252
01:24:15.745 --> 01:24:17.163
Mercer, it's me.

1253
01:24:17.246 --> 01:24:18.497
You're surrounded by friends.

1254
01:24:22.501 --> 01:24:24.462
Mercer, stop.

1255
01:24:47.693 --> 01:24:49.361
How you doing, sweetie?

1256
01:24:51.864 --> 01:24:54.492
You've been in bed for three days.

1257
01:24:55.659 --> 01:24:58.496
Come out in the garden with me today, okay?

1258
01:24:58.579 --> 01:25:01.916
I've got some new snapdragons
that need to be planted.

1259
01:25:03.209 --> 01:25:04.210
Okay?

1260
01:25:05.419 --> 01:25:06.420
Honey.

1261
01:25:09.673 --> 01:25:10.674
Oh, honey.

1262
01:25:18.516 --> 01:25:20.976
<i>The California Department of Motor Vehicles</i>

1263
01:25:21.060 --> 01:25:23.687
<i>has proposed regulation</i>
<i>that self-driving cars</i>

1264
01:25:23.771 --> 01:25:26.732
<i>should be required to have</i>
<i>a licensed driver inside.</i>

1265
01:25:26.816 --> 01:25:28.234
<i>Is that a good principle?</i>

1266
01:25:28.317 --> 01:25:31.153
<i>Yeah, I mean, I think obviously,</i>

1267
01:25:31.237 --> 01:25:33.739
<i>where technology is today</i>
<i>that is a good principle,</i>

1268
01:25:33.823 --> 01:25:36.158
<i>and of course, we would not suggest</i>
<i>putting something unsafe on the road.</i>

1269
01:25:36.242 --> 01:25:37.701
<i>That's why we have fed...</i>

1270
01:27:33.901 --> 01:27:34.735
<i>Mae.</i>

1271
01:27:35.945 --> 01:27:37.321
Where are you?

1272
01:27:37.404 --> 01:27:40.115
- Are we alone?
- Yes.

1273
01:27:40.199 --> 01:27:41.200
Are we?

1274
01:27:41.283 --> 01:27:42.284
Yes.

1275
01:27:44.495 --> 01:27:47.456
How is it daytime where you are?

1276
01:27:47.539 --> 01:27:49.917
Wait, you're in Scotland.

1277
01:27:50.000 --> 01:27:52.503
Yep, I went home. Can you see?

1278
01:27:54.463 --> 01:27:55.923
What happened?

1279
01:27:56.006 --> 01:27:58.801
What, besides almost ODing
on speed and Soylent?

1280
01:27:58.884 --> 01:28:00.636
Well, I found out I'm allergic to wheat.

1281
01:28:04.348 --> 01:28:07.226
Um... I don't know.

1282
01:28:07.309 --> 01:28:08.602
I guess I almost died?

1283
01:28:12.398 --> 01:28:14.358
Oh, come on, no, don't. Don't make me cry.

1284
01:28:14.441 --> 01:28:16.860
Come on. No. I'm not going to cry anymore.

1285
01:28:19.071 --> 01:28:19.947
You getting better there?

1286
01:28:21.156 --> 01:28:23.200
<i>Yeah, I am.</i>

1287
01:28:23.284 --> 01:28:28.914
I eat. I look out the window.
I come out here, and I think I daydream.

1288
01:28:28.998 --> 01:28:30.082
Hadn't done that in years.

1289
01:28:31.583 --> 01:28:32.668
I'm glad you called.

1290
01:28:35.796 --> 01:28:37.298
I didn't want to lose you, too.

1291
01:28:38.841 --> 01:28:39.842
<i>I know.</i>

1292
01:28:41.635 --> 01:28:43.929
Listen, Mae, I'm really,
really sorry about Mercer.

1293
01:28:44.972 --> 01:28:47.808
And I'm a shit friend.
I should've said something sooner.

1294
01:28:47.891 --> 01:28:49.268
I should've, and I'm sorry.

1295
01:28:51.020 --> 01:28:52.229
It's just so horrible.

1296
01:28:54.898 --> 01:28:56.400
Have they spoken to you about it?

1297
01:28:57.735 --> 01:28:58.736
Not yet.

1298
01:28:59.903 --> 01:29:01.530
I'm going in tomorrow.

1299
01:29:01.613 --> 01:29:04.867
<i>Uh-huh. They ask for forgiveness</i>
<i>and never for permission.</i>

1300
01:29:04.950 --> 01:29:07.036
They have to go.
They've broken a hundred laws.

1301
01:29:07.119 --> 01:29:10.122
They've made me suffer
a thousand regulations.

1302
01:29:10.205 --> 01:29:11.749
There's got to be a way to...

1303
01:29:11.832 --> 01:29:14.084
What? Stop people doing bad shit?

1304
01:29:15.168 --> 01:29:16.211
Yeah.

1305
01:29:17.588 --> 01:29:20.799
Do you need some time away here, too?
I've got an extra bed if you want it.

1306
01:29:25.346 --> 01:29:29.266
This feels so good, just being with you.

1307
01:29:29.350 --> 01:29:31.894
- Even like this.
- Yeah.

1308
01:29:33.020 --> 01:29:34.229
Yeah. I know.

1309
01:29:37.524 --> 01:29:39.735
This is going to sound crazy. But...

1310
01:29:39.818 --> 01:29:41.862
I keep having this feeling that...

1311
01:29:42.905 --> 01:29:45.282
Mercer would still be here
if we hadn't lost touch.

1312
01:29:45.366 --> 01:29:47.326
No, come on, what were you supposed to do?

1313
01:29:47.409 --> 01:29:49.703
I mean, you know, he drifted away.

1314
01:29:50.704 --> 01:29:51.830
It happens.

1315
01:29:51.914 --> 01:29:55.000
Yes, but we have this technology,
all this power.

1316
01:29:55.084 --> 01:29:58.462
Well, I mean, technology
didn't exactly help the situation.

1317
01:30:00.464 --> 01:30:01.465
What if it could?

1318
01:30:07.846 --> 01:30:10.724
Hey, your dad woke up at 4:00 this morning,

1319
01:30:10.808 --> 01:30:12.643
and your light was still on.

1320
01:30:12.726 --> 01:30:15.312
What were you doing in your room?
Were you on the phone?

1321
01:30:17.064 --> 01:30:18.649
It helped.

1322
01:30:18.732 --> 01:30:20.567
I have friends all over the world.

1323
01:30:21.985 --> 01:30:22.986
They care.

1324
01:30:23.070 --> 01:30:24.404
Mm.

1325
01:30:24.488 --> 01:30:28.075
- I spoke to Annie.
- How is she?

1326
01:30:28.158 --> 01:30:29.785
Good. Better.

1327
01:30:30.953 --> 01:30:32.621
I realized how much I missed her.

1328
01:30:36.917 --> 01:30:38.669
I have to go back.

1329
01:30:38.752 --> 01:30:40.170
Go back where?

1330
01:30:42.589 --> 01:30:43.715
Not to the Circle.

1331
01:30:43.799 --> 01:30:45.843
The Circle didn't kill Mercer, Mom.

1332
01:30:45.926 --> 01:30:48.470
It was a bad tool, a bad system.

1333
01:30:48.554 --> 01:30:51.473
It... It can be fixed.

1334
01:30:52.933 --> 01:30:55.519
We're not gonna become Amish, Mom.

1335
01:30:55.602 --> 01:30:58.230
I... When someone dies in a plane crash,

1336
01:30:58.313 --> 01:31:02.234
you don't abandon planes,
you make them safer.

1337
01:31:02.317 --> 01:31:03.986
Mae, at least wait a bit.

1338
01:31:04.069 --> 01:31:05.946
I'm the only one who can do this.

1339
01:31:07.447 --> 01:31:08.448
I'm sorry.

1340
01:31:10.117 --> 01:31:11.618
I love you.

1341
01:31:35.142 --> 01:31:37.436
- <i>Hello?</i>
- <i>Hey, it's me.</i>

1342
01:31:37.519 --> 01:31:40.397
<i>Mae. How are you holding up?</i>

1343
01:31:40.480 --> 01:31:43.442
<i> Better, actually.</i>
<i>I'm coming in to see them today.</i>

1344
01:31:43.525 --> 01:31:46.862
- <i>But I need your help.</i>
- <i>Sure. Name it, anything you need.</i>

1345
01:32:16.558 --> 01:32:18.894
We were worried when we didn't
hear from you for so long.

1346
01:32:18.977 --> 01:32:20.729
We were very concerned.

1347
01:32:20.812 --> 01:32:23.106
No contact for four days.
Your camera was off.

1348
01:32:23.190 --> 01:32:24.066
How are you doing?

1349
01:32:27.319 --> 01:32:28.153
I'm okay.

1350
01:32:29.404 --> 01:32:31.365
Tom and I have been talking
about how little we knew

1351
01:32:31.448 --> 01:32:33.367
about this young man Mercer.

1352
01:32:33.450 --> 01:32:35.661
He's someone we should
have been able to help.

1353
01:32:35.744 --> 01:32:38.497
A fund should be established for his family.

1354
01:32:39.623 --> 01:32:42.125
Yeah, absolutely, that's done.

1355
01:32:42.209 --> 01:32:45.170
Listen, Mae, we would like
to offer you a new role

1356
01:32:45.254 --> 01:32:49.925
here at the Circle with no formal obligation,

1357
01:32:50.008 --> 01:32:51.969
maybe a lighter schedule.

1358
01:32:52.052 --> 01:32:54.554
You can choose your own projects,
and you can think long-term.

1359
01:32:54.638 --> 01:32:56.974
You could take pottery, if you wanted.

1360
01:32:57.057 --> 01:32:58.517
I don't know if you're aware of this,

1361
01:32:58.600 --> 01:33:01.353
- but one of our founders, Ty Lafitte...
- Yeah.

1362
01:33:01.436 --> 01:33:06.733
...was given the same opportunity,
and now he loves his freedom.

1363
01:33:06.817 --> 01:33:10.028
And I don't know if this is any consolation,

1364
01:33:10.112 --> 01:33:13.490
but at our next Dream Friday...

1365
01:33:13.573 --> 01:33:15.450
We're going to imagine a world

1366
01:33:15.534 --> 01:33:19.246
in which every disturbed young man
could be kept safe.

1367
01:33:20.956 --> 01:33:22.249
Would you like to join us?

1368
01:33:24.376 --> 01:33:27.004
I'm not sure I'm ready for that. But...

1369
01:33:28.964 --> 01:33:30.048
thanks for the invitation.

1370
01:33:31.049 --> 01:33:31.883
Take your time.

1371
01:33:57.701 --> 01:34:01.079
I did what you asked.
You won't believe the shit I found.

1372
01:34:01.163 --> 01:34:05.584
It's worse than I could've imagined.
And, and look, Mae, I'm so sorry.

1373
01:34:05.667 --> 01:34:09.546
I never met him, but they should build
a monument to Mercer.

1374
01:34:11.798 --> 01:34:15.469
- Yeah.
- I mean, this... this changes everything.

1375
01:34:15.552 --> 01:34:18.597
Okay, he's the martyr
who will wake everyone up, right?

1376
01:34:18.680 --> 01:34:22.142
I don't know what you're planning,
but if you turn that camera back on,

1377
01:34:22.225 --> 01:34:25.145
whatever you say about the Circle
is heard around the world,

1378
01:34:25.228 --> 01:34:26.855
and it changes everything.

1379
01:34:42.245 --> 01:34:43.080
<i>Hello</i>.

1380
01:34:46.917 --> 01:34:47.918
Hello again.

1381
01:34:53.632 --> 01:34:54.966
- <i>Hi!</i>
- <i>Hey, Mae.</i>

1382
01:35:04.434 --> 01:35:06.144
And that is how it should have been,

1383
01:35:06.228 --> 01:35:08.730
how it could've been,
but we didn't have the technology.

1384
01:35:08.814 --> 01:35:10.065
But we will.

1385
01:35:10.148 --> 01:35:11.525
And when we do,

1386
01:35:11.608 --> 01:35:14.903
when every vehicle is known and connected,

1387
01:35:14.986 --> 01:35:18.323
the data we have will help us
improve traffic patterns,

1388
01:35:18.407 --> 01:35:24.996
keep drivers safe, and optimize
Circle services across all platforms.

1389
01:35:25.080 --> 01:35:28.208
Shopping. Lodging.

1390
01:35:28.291 --> 01:35:29.835
Attractions.

1391
01:35:30.877 --> 01:35:32.546
Attractions like Mae Holland.

1392
01:35:32.629 --> 01:35:34.297
Mae, come on out here.

1393
01:35:34.381 --> 01:35:36.758
<i>Come on out here. Gosh, Mae.</i>

1394
01:35:36.842 --> 01:35:39.177
<i>Oh, you're transparent again. Look at that.</i>

1395
01:35:39.261 --> 01:35:41.430
Hello, hello.

1396
01:35:41.513 --> 01:35:42.472
Mae Holland.

1397
01:35:43.556 --> 01:35:46.059
Welcome back, Mae!

1398
01:35:46.143 --> 01:35:51.523
Now, Mae is a brave person

1399
01:35:51.606 --> 01:35:53.442
because even amid her grieving,

1400
01:35:53.525 --> 01:35:58.363
she knows that she has a responsibility
to her followers,

1401
01:35:58.447 --> 01:35:59.990
to the whole world, really.

1402
01:36:01.575 --> 01:36:05.996
Thanks for saying that, Eamon.
And I couldn't agree with you more.

1403
01:36:07.414 --> 01:36:10.333
I know that Tom, he agrees, too.

1404
01:36:10.417 --> 01:36:13.504
Right, Tom? You want to come out?

1405
01:36:14.588 --> 01:36:17.215
- Come on.
- There you are.

1406
01:36:21.178 --> 01:36:24.681
First, I want to thank everyone here
and around the world

1407
01:36:24.764 --> 01:36:28.101
for the support you've given me
since we lost Mercer.

1408
01:36:28.185 --> 01:36:32.939
These past days,
I've realized something about myself

1409
01:36:33.023 --> 01:36:34.191
and about the Circle.

1410
01:36:35.358 --> 01:36:37.694
It's that I don't want to be disconnected.

1411
01:36:39.112 --> 01:36:43.074
I don't want to have to search
for friends, family, my loved ones.

1412
01:36:44.284 --> 01:36:45.785
We lost track of Mercer.

1413
01:36:47.329 --> 01:36:49.915
But what if we never lost touch
with each other?

1414
01:36:52.125 --> 01:36:53.919
What if instead of searching...

1415
01:36:55.253 --> 01:36:56.421
we always knew?

1416
01:36:58.548 --> 01:37:03.470
What I am proposing is radical openness
and unbroken connection.

1417
01:37:06.223 --> 01:37:08.183
It would have to start here.

1418
01:37:10.018 --> 01:37:13.396
You know the usual hypocrisy
of the digital world.

1419
01:37:13.480 --> 01:37:17.651
We want everything in the Cloud,
and yet our leaders...

1420
01:37:19.611 --> 01:37:21.446
live above it.

1421
01:37:21.530 --> 01:37:26.159
So I want to invite Eamon and Tom

1422
01:37:26.243 --> 01:37:30.622
to join me in this experiment
that they've pioneered.

1423
01:37:32.249 --> 01:37:38.004
I invite them to set an example
for the Circle and the world...

1424
01:37:41.091 --> 01:37:42.634
to go transparent.

1425
01:37:47.347 --> 01:37:48.932
What do you say, guys?

1426
01:37:50.934 --> 01:37:51.935
Will you join me?

1427
01:37:59.067 --> 01:38:03.071
Wouldn't it be great to know
their every move, their every word?

1428
01:38:03.154 --> 01:38:07.534
From now on,
every message, meeting, phone call,

1429
01:38:07.617 --> 01:38:12.872
all will be public, knowable, never erased.

1430
01:38:14.040 --> 01:38:16.209
In fact, Ty Lafitte has helped us
make every document

1431
01:38:16.293 --> 01:38:19.921
in the company's history public.

1432
01:38:20.005 --> 01:38:22.549
Every message, every plan including...

1433
01:38:22.632 --> 01:38:25.885
And this is my favorite part, every e-mail,

1434
01:38:25.969 --> 01:38:30.015
from Tom and Eamon's accounts,
and their private accounts...

1435
01:38:31.016 --> 01:38:33.143
...and their secondary confidential accounts.

1436
01:38:33.226 --> 01:38:37.188
And even their super-secret
scrambled code accounts,

1437
01:38:37.272 --> 01:38:43.194
that nobody, not even their assistants
or wives knew existed.

1438
01:38:44.696 --> 01:38:45.822
Until now.

1439
01:38:48.783 --> 01:38:50.660
It's all been sent to you already.

1440
01:38:54.331 --> 01:38:56.499
We are so fucked.

1441
01:38:56.583 --> 01:39:00.503
I think we're entitled
to that kind of access. Don't you?

1442
01:39:00.587 --> 01:39:02.041
Yeah.

1443
01:39:05.133 --> 01:39:07.302
Mae, you are a very brave woman to do this.

1444
01:39:13.183 --> 01:39:16.269
There are no more secrets.

1445
01:39:16.353 --> 01:39:18.104
Privacy was a temporary thing.

1446
01:39:19.314 --> 01:39:20.398
And now it's over.

1447
01:39:22.150 --> 01:39:24.319
We won't live in the shadows anymore.

1448
01:39:25.737 --> 01:39:27.947
Hey. Good timing.

1449
01:39:47.509 --> 01:39:48.510
Thank you.

1450
01:39:49.761 --> 01:39:51.429
Thank you.

1451
01:39:51.513 --> 01:39:58.478
I'm tired of hiding,
these passwords and secret knocks,

1452
01:39:58.561 --> 01:40:01.356
the difference between public and private.

1453
01:40:02.982 --> 01:40:04.025
Aren't you all?

1454
01:40:05.443 --> 01:40:07.404
Come to think of it,

1455
01:40:07.487 --> 01:40:09.406
I'm tired of standing here in the dark.

1456
01:40:11.366 --> 01:40:12.575
Don't worry.

1457
01:40:12.659 --> 01:40:17.455
Eamon will catch up. Or maybe he won't.

1458
01:40:17.539 --> 01:40:20.291
Either way, it's time for a change.

1459
01:40:20.375 --> 01:40:22.293
We're gonna remake this place.

1460
01:40:22.377 --> 01:40:27.966
And if we do it right,
we will set an example for everyone.

1461
01:40:28.049 --> 01:40:29.634
The future won't wait.

1462
01:41:13.845 --> 01:41:14.846
<i>Hello.</i>

1463
01:41:57.472 --> 01:42:01.518
